WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.250
- Welcome to the Huberman Lab Podcast,

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.890
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:05.939
for everyday life.

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:09.150
[lively music]

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.170
I'm Andrew Huberman,

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.170
and I'm a professor of
neurobiology and ophthalmology

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.090
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.730
Today, my guest is Dr. Erich Jarvis.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.520
Dr. Jarvis is a professor at
the Rockefeller University

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:21.930
in New York City

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.580
and his laboratory studies,

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.670
the neurobiology of
vocal learning, language,

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:28.080
speech disorders,

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.870
and remarkably, the
relationship between language,

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:34.530
music and movement, in particular dance.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.200
His work spans from genomics,

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.860
so the very genes that make up our genome

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.690
and the genomes of other species

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:44.280
that speak and have language

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.860
such as songbirds and parrots

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.420
all the way up to neural circuits,

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.700
that is the connections
in the brain and body

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.530
that govern our ability to learn

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:54.210
and generate specific sounds

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.220
and movements coordinated
with those sounds,

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.170
including hand movements

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:01.290
and all the way up to cognition,

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.050
that is our ability to
think in specific ways,

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:05.550
based on what we are saying

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.350
and the way that we comprehend

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:10.860
what other people are
saying, singing and doing.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.360
As you'll soon see,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.670
I was immediately transfixed

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:18.030
and absolutely enchanted
by Dr. Jarvis's description

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.280
of his work and the ways that it impacts

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.530
all the various aspects of our lives.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:26.310
For instance, I learned from
Dr. Jarvis that as we read,

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.650
we are generating very low-levels

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:30.960
of motor activity in our throat.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.380
That is, we are speaking the
words that we are reading

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.930
at a level below the perception of sound

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.300
or our own perception of those words.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.890
But if one were to put an amplifier

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.950
or to measure the firing of those muscles

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:45.240
in our vocal chords,

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.250
we'd find that as we're
reading information,

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.830
we are actually speaking that information.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:51.870
And as I learned, and you'll soon learn,

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:54.360
there's a direct link
between those species

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.390
in the world that have song and movement,

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.490
which many of us would
associate with dance

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:03.510
and our ability to learn and
generate complex language.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.540
So for people with speech
disorders like stutter,

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:07.890
or for people who are interested

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:09.900
in multiple language learning,

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.940
bilingual, trilingual, et cetera,

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.340
and frankly for anyone who is interested

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.060
in how we communicate through
words, written or spoken,

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:19.770
I'm certain today's episode is going to be

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.680
an especially interesting
and important one for you.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:24.780
Dr. Jarvis's work is so pioneering

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:27.210
that he has been awarded
truly countless awards.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.070
I'm not going to take our time to list off

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:32.190
all the various important
awards that he's received,

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.090
but I should point out

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:35.790
that in addition to being
a decorated professor

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:37.260
at the Rockefeller University,

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:38.400
he is also an investigator

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.170
with the Howard Hughes Medical Institute,

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.027
the so-called HHMI.

00:02:42.027 --> 00:02:43.830
And for those of you that don't know,

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.320
HHMI investigators are selected

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.050
on an extremely competitive basis

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:50.610
that they have to re-up,

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:53.130
that is they have to
recompete every five years.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:56.040
They actually receive a
grade every five years

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.320
that dictates whether or
not they are no longer

00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:59.940
a Howard Hughes investigator

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.270
or whether or not they can
advance to another five years

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:05.400
of funding for their important research.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.840
And indeed, Howard Hughes investigators

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.600
are selected not just for
the rigor of their work,

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.220
but for their pioneering spirit

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:15.450
and their ability to take on
high-risk, high-benefit work,

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:16.740
which is exactly the kind of work

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:20.220
that Dr. Jarvis has been
providing for decades now.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:22.530
Again, I think today's episode
is one of the more unique

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.000
and special episodes that we've had

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.170
on the Huberman Lab Podcast.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:27.690
I single it out because it really spans

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:29.490
from the basic to the applied

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:32.160
and Dr. Jarvis's story is
an especially unique one

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.070
in terms of how he arrived
at becoming a neurobiologist.

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:36.840
So for those of you that are interested

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.820
in personal journey and personal story,

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:42.720
Dr. Jarvis's is truly a
special and important one.

