00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:02.370
And now, for my discussion
with Dr. Erich Jarvis.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:04.080
Erich, it's so great to have you here.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:05.340
- Thank you.
- Yeah.

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:07.200
Very interested in learning from you

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:09.900
about speech and language.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.000
And even as I ask the question,

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.790
I realize that a lot of
people, including myself,

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:17.250
probably don't fully
appreciate the distinction

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:19.560
between speech and language, right?

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:21.450
Speech, I think of as the motor patterns,

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:23.970
the production of sound

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:25.740
that has meaning, hopefully.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:29.130
And language, of course,
comes in various languages

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:31.860
and varieties of ways of communicating.

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:34.740
But in terms of the study
of speech and language,

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:36.570
and thinking about how the brain

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:38.730
organizes speech and language,

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:40.410
what are the similarities?

00:08:40.410 --> 00:08:41.400
What are the differences?

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:43.170
How should we think about
speech and language?

00:08:43.170 --> 00:08:46.800
- Yeah, well, I'm glad you,
you know, inviting me here.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:50.130
And I'm also glad to
get that first question,

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:52.590
which I consider a provocative one.

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:55.440
The reason why, I've been struggling,

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:57.390
what is the difference
with speech and language

00:08:57.390 --> 00:08:58.680
for many years.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:00.650
And realize, why am I struggling,

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:03.120
is because there are behavioral terms,

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:04.920
let's call 'em psychologically,

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:06.850
psychology developed kind of terms

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:12.240
that don't actually align
exactly with brain function.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:13.073
All right.

00:09:13.073 --> 00:09:14.430
And the question is there a distinction

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:15.660
between speech and language?

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:17.430
And when I look at the brain

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:20.070
of work that other people
have done, work we have done,

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:21.900
also compared it with animal models,

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:23.400
like those who can imitate sounds

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:25.590
like parrots and songbirds.

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:30.210
I start to see there really
isn't such a sharp distinction.

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:32.370
So, to get at what I think is going on,

00:09:32.370 --> 00:09:35.700
let me tell you how some
people think of it now.

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:38.380
That there's a separate
language module in the brain

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:42.270
that has all the
algorithms and computations

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:44.280
that influence the speech pathway

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:46.320
on how to produce sound

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:49.110
and the auditory pathway
on how to perceive

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:51.930
and interpret it for speech

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:55.140
or for, you know, sound
that we call speech.

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:58.620
And it turns out,

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:00.600
I don't think there is any good evidence

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:02.550
for a separate language module.

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:07.230
Instead, there is a
speech production pathway

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:08.610
that's controlling our larynx,

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:10.500
controlling our jaw muscles

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:12.750
that has built within it

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:16.110
all the complex algorithms
for spoken language.

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:18.630
And there's the auditory pathway

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:19.980
that has built within it,

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:22.920
all the complex algorithms
for understanding speech,

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:25.260
not separate from a language module.

00:10:25.260 --> 00:10:27.570
And this speech production pathway

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:29.200
is specialized to humans

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:32.520
and parrots and songbirds,

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:35.220
whereas this auditory perception pathway

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:38.130
is more ubiquitous amongst
the animal kingdom.

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:39.847
And this is why dogs can understand,

00:10:39.847 --> 00:10:43.297
"sit", "sientese", "come here, boy",

00:10:43.297 --> 00:10:44.820
"get the ball" and so forth.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:48.540
Dogs can understand several
hundred human speech words.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.270
Great apes, you can teach
them for several thousand,

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:52.743
but they can't say a word.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:55.320
- Fascinating.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:10:58.080
Because you've raised a
number of animal species

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:00.900
early on here and because I have a,

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:02.340
basically an obsession with animals

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:03.570
since the time I was very small,

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:08.570
I have to ask, which
animals have language?

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:11.970
Which animals have modes of communication

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:14.215
that are sort of like language.

00:11:14.215 --> 00:11:15.048
- [Erich] Yeah.

00:11:15.048 --> 00:11:16.320
- You know, I've heard whale songs.

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:17.490
I don't know what they're saying.

00:11:17.490 --> 00:11:18.540
They sound very beautiful,

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:21.060
but they could be insulting
each other for all I know.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:21.893
- [Erich] Yeah.

00:11:21.893 --> 00:11:24.180
- And they very well may be.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:26.163
Dolphins, birds, I mean,

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:30.480
what do we understand about
modes of communication

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:32.790
that are like language,

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:36.390
but might not be what would
classically be called language?

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:37.800
- Yes, right.

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:40.560
So, modes of communication

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:43.320
that people would define as language,

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:46.200
more, very, in a very narrow definition,

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:49.023
they would say, production
of sound, so speech.

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:52.650
But what about the hands,
the gesturing with the hands?

00:11:52.650 --> 00:11:56.250
What about a bird who is doing
aerial displays in the air,

00:11:56.250 --> 00:12:00.660
communicating information
through body language, right.

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:03.750
Well, I'm going to go back to the brain.

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:06.750
So what I think is going on

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:08.970
is for spoken language,

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:10.950
we're using the speech pathway

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:13.020
and all the complex algorithms there.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:14.550
Next to the brain regions

00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:16.290
that are controlling spoken language

00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:19.530
are the brain regions for
gesturing with the hands.

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:22.260
And that hand parallel pathway

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:25.410
has also complex algorithms
that we can utilize.

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:28.590
And some species are more
advanced in these circuits,

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:31.320
whether it's sound or gesturing with hands

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:33.900
and some are less advanced.

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:35.940
Now, we, humans and a few others

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:39.870
are the most advanced
for the speech sounds

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:41.370
or the spoken language,

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:45.300
but a non-human primate
can produce gesturing

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:48.213
in a more advanced form than
they could produce sound.

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:51.390
I'm not sure I got that across clearly,

00:12:51.390 --> 00:12:54.990
just to say that humans
are the most advanced

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:56.970
at spoken language,

00:12:56.970 --> 00:13:01.290
but not necessarily as big a
difference at gestural language

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:03.300
compared to some of the species.

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:05.700
- Very clear and very interesting.

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:08.460
And immediately prompts the question,

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:09.960
have there been brain imaging

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:14.670
or other sorts of studies
evaluating neural activity

00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:18.150
in the context of, you know,
cultures and languages,

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:20.340
at least that I associate
with a lot of hand movement,

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:21.600
like Italian.
- Yep.

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:23.280
- Versus, I don't know,

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:25.200
maybe you could give us
some examples of cultures

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:27.900
where language is not associated with

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:29.490
as much overt hand movement.

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:32.730
- Yes, so as you and I
are talking here today

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:35.040
and people who are
listening, but can't see us,

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:38.110
we're actually gesturing
with our hands as we talk

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:42.480
without knowing it or
doing it unconsciously.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:45.060
And if we were talking on a telephone,

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:46.110
I would have one hand here

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:47.897
and I'd be gesturing with the other hand.

00:13:47.897 --> 00:13:48.730
[Andrew laughs]

00:13:48.730 --> 00:13:50.430
Without even you seeing me, right?

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:52.500
And so why is that?

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:54.210
Some have argued and I would agree,

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:56.160
but based upon what we've seen

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:58.740
is that there's an
evolutionary relationship

00:13:58.740 --> 00:14:00.030
between the brain pathways

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:03.393
that control speech
production and gesturing.

00:14:04.290 --> 00:14:05.940
And the brain regions I mentioned

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:07.440
are directly adjacent to each other.

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:08.490
And why is that?

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:11.130
I think that the brain
pathways that control speech

00:14:11.130 --> 00:14:15.390
evolved out of the brain pathways
that control body movement.

00:14:15.390 --> 00:14:16.223
All right.

00:14:16.223 --> 00:14:18.520
And that

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:22.050
when you talk about Italian, French,

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:23.283
English, and so forth,

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:25.680
each one of those languages

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:29.160
come with a learned set of gestures

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:31.140
that you can communicate with.

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:33.180
Now, how is that related to other animals?

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:35.370
Well, Cocoa, a gorilla who is raised

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:39.120
with humans for 39 years or more,

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:42.300
learned how to do gesture communication,

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:45.600
learned how to sign
language, so to speak, right?

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:48.060
But Cocoa couldn't produce those sounds.

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:50.380
Cocoa could understand them as well

00:14:51.595 --> 00:14:53.880
by seeing somebody sign

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:56.190
or hearing somebody produce speech,

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:58.740
but Cocoa couldn't
produce it with her voice.

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:02.550
And so, what's going on there is that

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:04.260
a number of species, not all of them,

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:07.140
a number of species have
motor pathways in the brain

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:09.450
where you can do learn gesturing,

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:12.660
rudimentary language, if you
wanted, say with your limbs,

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:14.790
even if it's not as advanced as humans.

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:18.750
But they don't have this extra
brain pathway for the sound.

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:21.120
So they can't gesture with their voice

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:23.520
in the way that they
gesture with their hands.

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:24.540
- I see.

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:27.030
One thing that I've wondered
about for a very long time

00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:28.030
is whether or not

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:34.170
primitive emotions and primitive sounds

00:15:34.170 --> 00:15:37.230
are the early substrate of language.

00:15:37.230 --> 00:15:39.150
And whether or not there's a bridge

00:15:39.150 --> 00:15:40.890
that we can draw between those

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:44.490
in terms of just the
basic respiration systems

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:47.430
associated with different
extreme feelings.

00:15:47.430 --> 00:15:50.580
Here's the way I'm
imagining this might work.

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:53.460
When I smell something delicious,

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:57.000
I typically inhale more.
- Hmm hmm.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:16:00.270
- And I might say, mmm,
or something like that.

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:02.040
Whereas if I smell something putrid,

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:04.230
I typically turn away, and I wince

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:06.120
and I will exhale [exhales],

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:07.890
you know or sort of
kind of like turn away,

00:16:07.890 --> 00:16:10.050
trying to not ingest those molecules

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:11.820
or inhale those molecules.

00:16:11.820 --> 00:16:13.680
I could imagine that these

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:17.070
are the basic dark and light contrasts

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:18.990
of the language system.

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:19.823
And as I say that,

00:16:19.823 --> 00:16:23.100
I'm saying that from the
orientation of a vision scientist

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:26.130
who thinks of all visual images built up

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:29.190
in a very basic way of
a hierarchical map model

00:16:29.190 --> 00:16:31.083
of the ability to see dark and light.

00:16:31.083 --> 00:16:33.390
So I could imagine this kind of primitive

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:37.320
to more sophisticated pyramid

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:40.410
of sound to language.

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:41.780
Is this a crazy idea?

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:45.420
Do we have any evidence
this is the way it works?

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:47.070
- No, it's not a crazy idea.

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:47.970
And in fact,

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:51.810
you hit upon one of the key distinctions

00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:54.450
in the field of research
that I started out in,

00:16:54.450 --> 00:16:56.490
which is vocal learning research.

00:16:56.490 --> 00:16:59.133
So for vocal communication,

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:03.630
you have most vertebrate species vocalize,

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:06.243
but most of them are
producing innate sounds

00:17:06.243 --> 00:17:10.200
that they're born with producing,

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:13.020
that is babies crying, for example,

00:17:13.020 --> 00:17:14.343
or dogs barking.

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:18.750
And only a few species have
learned vocal communication,

00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:20.730
the ability to imitate sounds.

00:17:20.730 --> 00:17:23.430
And that is what makes
spoken language special.

00:17:23.430 --> 00:17:26.670
When people think of what's
special about language,

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:29.280
it's the learned vocalizations.

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:31.140
That is what's rare.

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:34.780
And so, this distinction
between innateness and learned

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:38.400
is more of a bigger dichotomy

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:39.600
when it comes to vocalizations

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:42.870
than for other behaviors
in the animal kingdom.

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:45.480
And when you go in the brain,

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:46.730
you see it there as well.

00:17:47.805 --> 00:17:49.500
And so all the things you talked about,

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:51.870
the breathing, the grunting and so forth,

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:55.170
a lot of that is handled
by the brain stem circuits,

00:17:55.170 --> 00:17:57.970
you know, right around the
level of your neck and below.

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:00.930
Like a reflex kind of thing.

00:18:00.930 --> 00:18:04.650
So, or even some emotional
aspects of your behavior

00:18:04.650 --> 00:18:06.660
in the hypothalamus and so forth.

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:08.730
But for a learned behavior,

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:10.980
learning how to speak,

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:12.780
learning how to play the piano,

00:18:12.780 --> 00:18:15.540
teaching a dog to learn how to do tricks

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:18.090
is using the forebrain circuits.

00:18:18.090 --> 00:18:20.070
And what has happened is

00:18:20.070 --> 00:18:21.810
that there's a lot of forebrain circuits

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:22.643
that are controlling,

00:18:22.643 --> 00:18:24.540
learning how to move body
parts in these species,

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:26.340
but not for the vocalizations.

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:29.760
But in humans and in parrots
and in some other species,

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:32.040
somehow, we acquired circuits

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:36.270
where the forebrain has
taken over the brain stem

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:38.220
and now using that brain stem,

00:18:38.220 --> 00:18:41.370
not only to produce the innate
behaviors or vocal behaviors,

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:42.820
but the learned ones as well.

00:18:43.710 --> 00:18:44.760
- Do we have any sense

00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:49.760
of when modern or
sophisticated language evolved?

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:54.330
You know, thinking back to the
species that we evolved from,

00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:56.673
and even within Homo sapiens,

00:18:58.110 --> 00:18:59.550
has there been an evolution of language?

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:02.160
Has there been a devolution
of language? [laughs]

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:03.300
- Yeah.

00:19:03.300 --> 00:19:04.983
Yeah, I would say,

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:08.940
and to be able to answer that question,

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:10.440
it does come with the caveat

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:13.350
that I think we humans overrate ourselves

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:15.060
compared to other species.

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:20.010
And so it makes even scientists go astray

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:21.240
in trying to hypothesize

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:23.640
when, you especially
don't find fossil evidence

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:24.790
of language that easily

00:19:26.790 --> 00:19:30.543
out there in terms of
what happened in the past.

00:19:33.150 --> 00:19:35.820
Amongst the primates,
which we humans belong to,

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:36.843
we are the only ones that

00:19:36.843 --> 00:19:39.603
have this advanced vocal learning ability.

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:45.513
Now, it was assumed that
it was only Homo sapiens,

00:19:46.830 --> 00:19:48.990
then you can go back in time now

00:19:48.990 --> 00:19:51.030
based upon genomic data,

00:19:51.030 --> 00:19:52.500
not only of us living humans,

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:54.600
but of the fossils that have been found

00:19:54.600 --> 00:19:59.600
for Homo sapiens, of Neanderthals,
of Denisovan individuals

00:20:00.270 --> 00:20:05.270
and discover that our
ancestor, our human ancestors,

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:10.550
supposedly hybridized with
these other hominid species.

00:20:10.830 --> 00:20:13.680
And it was assumed that
these other hominid species

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:15.573
don't learn how to imitate sounds.

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:18.450
I don't know of any species today

00:20:18.450 --> 00:20:21.150
that's a vocal learner
that can have children

00:20:21.150 --> 00:20:23.340
with a non-vocal learning species.

00:20:23.340 --> 00:20:24.180
I don't see it.

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:26.100
Doesn't mean it didn't exist.

00:20:26.100 --> 00:20:28.920
And when we look at the genetic data

00:20:28.920 --> 00:20:33.000
from these ancestral
hominids, that, you know,

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:35.550
where we can look at
genes that are involved

00:20:35.550 --> 00:20:37.530
in learned vocal communication,

00:20:37.530 --> 00:20:41.070
they have the same
sequence as we humans do

00:20:41.070 --> 00:20:43.800
for genes that function
in speech circuits.

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:46.380
So I think Neanderthals
had spoken language.

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:48.990
I'm not going to say it's as
advanced as what it is in humans.

00:20:48.990 --> 00:20:50.280
I don't know.

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:52.260
But I think it's been there for at least

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:55.410
between 500,000 to a million years

00:20:55.410 --> 00:20:58.560
that our ancestors had this ability

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:00.960
and that we've been coming
more and more advanced with it

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:03.393
culturally and possibly genetically.

00:21:04.530 --> 00:21:06.000
But I think it's evolved sometime

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:08.280
in the last 500,000 to a million years.

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:09.113
- Incredible.

00:21:10.710 --> 00:21:11.910
Maybe we could talk a little bit more

00:21:11.910 --> 00:21:14.460
about the overlap between brain circuits

00:21:14.460 --> 00:21:16.620
that control language and speech

00:21:16.620 --> 00:21:18.693
in humans and other animals.

00:21:20.250 --> 00:21:23.250
I was weaned in the neuroscience era

00:21:23.250 --> 00:21:27.570
where birdsong and the ability of birds

00:21:27.570 --> 00:21:29.639
to learn their tooter song

00:21:29.639 --> 00:21:32.610
was and still is a prominent field,

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:33.900
subfield of neuroscience.

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:34.733
And then of course,

00:21:34.733 --> 00:21:39.510
neuroimaging of humans speaking
and learning, et cetera,

00:21:39.510 --> 00:21:41.160
and this notion of a critical period,

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:44.400
a time in which language
is learned more easily

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:46.200
than it is later in life.

00:21:46.200 --> 00:21:48.810
And the names of the different brain areas

00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:50.490
were quite different.

00:21:50.490 --> 00:21:52.140
If one opens the textbooks,

00:21:52.140 --> 00:21:54.630
we hear Wernicke's and
Broca's for the humans.

00:21:54.630 --> 00:21:55.920
And you look at the birds of it,

00:21:55.920 --> 00:21:57.303
I remember, you know.

00:21:57.303 --> 00:21:59.970
- HVC.
- Robustus, striatum.

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:00.870
Area X.
- That's right.

00:22:00.870 --> 00:22:02.790
That's right, yes.
- Et cetera.

00:22:02.790 --> 00:22:04.590
But for most of our listeners,

00:22:04.590 --> 00:22:06.990
those names won't mean a whole lot,

00:22:06.990 --> 00:22:10.890
but in terms of homologies

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:13.620
between areas in terms of
function, what do we know?

00:22:13.620 --> 00:22:17.250
And how similar or different
are the brain areas

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:18.677
controlling speech and language

00:22:18.677 --> 00:22:22.410
in say a songbird and a young human child?

00:22:22.410 --> 00:22:26.100
- Yeah, so, going back to the 1950s

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:27.330
or even a little earlier,

00:22:27.330 --> 00:22:31.470
and Peter Muller and others who
got involved in neurotology,

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:34.800
the study of neurobiology of behavior

00:22:34.800 --> 00:22:36.303
in a natural way, right.

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:40.860
You know, they start to
find that behaviorally,

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:42.300
there are these species of birds

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:43.590
like songbirds and parrots,

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:46.050
and now we also know
hummingbirds, just three of them

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:48.990
out of the 40-something bird
groups out there on the planet,

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:52.680
orders, that they can
imitate sounds like we do.

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:54.360
And so that was a similarity.

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:56.220
In other words, they had
this kind of behavior

00:22:56.220 --> 00:22:59.280
that's more similar to us
than chimpanzees have with us

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:01.680
or than chickens have with them, right,

00:23:01.680 --> 00:23:03.240
their closer relatives.

00:23:03.240 --> 00:23:05.670
And then they discovered
even more similarities,

00:23:05.670 --> 00:23:10.170
these critical periods
that if you remove a child,

00:23:10.170 --> 00:23:13.350
you know, this unfortunately
happens where a child is feral

00:23:13.350 --> 00:23:14.700
and is not raised with human

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:18.000
and goes through their
puberty phase of growth,

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:21.240
it becomes hard for them to
learn a language as an adult.

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:24.060
So there's this critical
period where you learn best.

00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:26.400
And even later on, when
you're in regular society,

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:27.540
it's hard to learn.

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:30.450
Well, the birds undergo these same thing.

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:33.990
And then it was discovered
that if they become deaf,

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:35.610
we humans become deaf,

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:37.500
our speech starts to deteriorate

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:39.063
without any kind of therapy.

00:23:40.020 --> 00:23:43.230
If a non-human primate or, you know,

00:23:43.230 --> 00:23:46.530
or let's say a chicken becomes deaf,

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:48.420
their vocalizations don't deteriorate.

00:23:48.420 --> 00:23:50.400
very little at least.

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:52.920
Well, this happens in
the vocal learning birds.

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:54.810
So there were all these
behavioral parallels

00:23:54.810 --> 00:23:56.580
that came along with a package,

00:23:56.580 --> 00:23:58.950
and then people looked into the brain.

00:23:58.950 --> 00:24:01.530
Fernando Nottebohm, my former PhD advisor,

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:05.340
and began to discover the
Area X you talked about,

00:24:05.340 --> 00:24:07.563
the robust nucleus of the archipallium.

00:24:09.900 --> 00:24:12.000
And these brain pathways were not found

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:13.350
in the species who couldn't imitate

00:24:13.350 --> 00:24:15.000
so there was a parallel here.

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:18.420
And then jumping many
years later, you know,

00:24:18.420 --> 00:24:21.390
I started to dig down
into these brain circuits

00:24:21.390 --> 00:24:23.790
to discover that these brain circuits

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:26.790
had parallel functions with
the brain circuits for humans,

00:24:26.790 --> 00:24:28.230
even though they're by a different name,

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:30.840
like Broca's laryngeal motor cortex.

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:32.310
And most recently,

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:34.647
we discovered not only
the actual circuitry

00:24:34.647 --> 00:24:36.510
and the connectivity are similar,

00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:39.180
but the underlying
genes that are expressed

00:24:39.180 --> 00:24:41.850
in these brain regions
in a specialized way,

00:24:41.850 --> 00:24:43.050
different from the rest of the brain,

00:24:43.050 --> 00:24:46.830
are also similar between humans,
and songbirds and parrots.

00:24:46.830 --> 00:24:48.150
So all the way down to the genes.

00:24:48.150 --> 00:24:51.300
And now we're finding
the specific mutations

00:24:51.300 --> 00:24:54.033
are also similar, not always
identical, but similar,

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:57.060
which indicates remarkable convergence

00:24:57.060 --> 00:24:59.400
for a so-called complex behavior

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:01.560
in species separated by 300 million years

00:25:01.560 --> 00:25:02.910
from a common ancestor.

00:25:02.910 --> 00:25:04.080
And not only that,

00:25:04.080 --> 00:25:08.340
we are discovering that
mutations in these genes

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:12.153
that cause speech deficits
in humans, like in FOXP2,

00:25:13.351 --> 00:25:15.210
if you put those same mutations

00:25:15.210 --> 00:25:18.060
or similar type of deficits
in these vocal learning birds,

00:25:18.060 --> 00:25:19.740
you get similar deficits.

00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:21.900
So convergence of the behavior

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:24.750
is associated with
similar genetic disorders

00:25:24.750 --> 00:25:25.620
of the behavior.

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:26.880
- Incredible.

00:25:26.880 --> 00:25:31.020
I have to ask, do hummingbird
sing, or do they hum?

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:32.940
- Hummingbirds hum with their wings

00:25:32.940 --> 00:25:34.700
and sing with their syrinx.

00:25:34.700 --> 00:25:36.110
- In a coordinated way?

00:25:36.110 --> 00:25:37.560
- In a coordinated way.

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:42.243
There's some species of
hummingbirds that actually will,

00:25:43.110 --> 00:25:44.490
Doug Ashler showed this,

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:46.980
that will flap their wings

00:25:46.980 --> 00:25:49.980
and create a slapping
sound with their wings

00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:52.290
that's in unison with their song

00:25:52.290 --> 00:25:54.210
and you would not know it,

00:25:54.210 --> 00:25:57.543
but it sounds like a particular
syllable in their songs,

00:25:58.560 --> 00:26:00.330
even though it's their wings

00:26:00.330 --> 00:26:02.070
and their voice at the same time.

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:04.470
- Hummingbirds are clapping to their song?

00:26:04.470 --> 00:26:05.520
- Clapping with their,

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:08.220
they're snapping their wings together

00:26:08.220 --> 00:26:10.860
in unison with a song to make it like,

00:26:10.860 --> 00:26:14.310
if I'm going ba, da, da,
da [bangs], but, da [bangs]

00:26:14.310 --> 00:26:15.750
and I banged on the table

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:18.090
except they make it almost
sound like their voice

00:26:18.090 --> 00:26:19.023
with their wings.

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:21.300
- Incredible.
- Yes.

00:26:21.300 --> 00:26:22.620
- I, I'm...

00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:23.453
- And they got some of the
smallest brains around.

00:26:23.453 --> 00:26:25.740
- As the kids would say mind blown, right?

00:26:25.740 --> 00:26:26.573
- Yes. Yes.

00:26:26.573 --> 00:26:27.510
- Incredible.
- Yes.

00:26:27.510 --> 00:26:28.710
- Incredible, I love hummingbirds.

00:26:28.710 --> 00:26:31.080
And I always feel like
it's such a special thing

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:32.130
to get a moment to see one

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:33.390
because they move around so fast

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:36.660
and they fled away so fast in
these ballistic trajectories.

00:26:36.660 --> 00:26:37.493
- [Erich] Yep.

00:26:37.493 --> 00:26:40.680
- That when you get to see
one stationary for a moment,

00:26:40.680 --> 00:26:42.930
or even just hovering there,

00:26:42.930 --> 00:26:44.370
you feel like you're extracting so much

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:47.130
from their little microcosm of life,

00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:49.680
but now I realize they're
playing music essentially.

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:50.513
- Right, exactly.

00:26:50.513 --> 00:26:53.010
And what's amazing about hummingbirds

00:26:53.010 --> 00:26:56.310
and I'm going to say, vocal
learning species in general,

00:26:56.310 --> 00:26:58.200
is that for whatever reason,

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:01.920
they seem to evolve
multiple complex traits.

00:27:01.920 --> 00:27:04.860
You know, this idea that
the evolving language,

00:27:04.860 --> 00:27:06.840
spoken language in particular,

00:27:06.840 --> 00:27:09.690
comes along with a set of specializations.

00:27:09.690 --> 00:27:11.280
- Incredible.
- Yeah.

00:27:11.280 --> 00:27:13.620
- When I was coming up in neuroscience,

00:27:13.620 --> 00:27:17.550
I learned that I think it
was the work of Peter Muller

00:27:17.550 --> 00:27:20.550
that young birds learn,

00:27:20.550 --> 00:27:25.550
songbirds learned their tooter
song and learn it quite well,

00:27:26.310 --> 00:27:29.490
but that they could learn
the song of another tooter.

00:27:29.490 --> 00:27:31.170
In other words, they
could learn a different,

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:32.767
and for the listeners,
I'm doing air quotes here,

00:27:32.767 --> 00:27:33.817
"a different language",

00:27:33.817 --> 00:27:35.610
"a different bird song",

00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:37.590
different than their own species song.

00:27:37.590 --> 00:27:39.660
But never as well as they could learn

00:27:39.660 --> 00:27:44.430
their own natural genetically linked song.

00:27:44.430 --> 00:27:45.450
- Yes.
- Genetically linked,

00:27:45.450 --> 00:27:47.130
meaning that it would be like

00:27:47.130 --> 00:27:48.840
me being raised in a different culture,

00:27:48.840 --> 00:27:52.920
and that I would learn the other language,

00:27:52.920 --> 00:27:55.710
but not as well as I would
have learned English.

00:27:55.710 --> 00:27:57.060
This is the idea.
- Yes.

00:27:57.060 --> 00:27:59.051
- Is that true?
- That is true, yes.

00:27:59.051 --> 00:28:01.170
And that's what I learned
growing up as well.

00:28:01.170 --> 00:28:04.533
And talked to Peter Muller
himself about before he passed.

00:28:05.790 --> 00:28:09.510
Yeah, he used to call it the
innate predisposition to learn.

00:28:09.510 --> 00:28:10.941
All right.

00:28:10.941 --> 00:28:13.860
So which would be kind of the equivalent

00:28:13.860 --> 00:28:16.410
in the linguistic community
of universal grammar.

00:28:16.410 --> 00:28:19.710
There is something genetically

00:28:19.710 --> 00:28:23.010
influencing our vocal communication

00:28:23.010 --> 00:28:25.500
on top of what we learned culturally.

00:28:25.500 --> 00:28:27.240
And so there's this balance

00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:29.760
between the genetic control of speech

00:28:29.760 --> 00:28:31.410
or a song in these birds

00:28:31.410 --> 00:28:34.500
and the learned cultural control.

00:28:34.500 --> 00:28:37.293
And so, yes, if you were to take,

00:28:38.220 --> 00:28:40.500
you know, I mean, in this case,

00:28:40.500 --> 00:28:42.960
we actually tried this
at Rockefeller later on.

00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:46.620
Take a zebra finch and
raise it with a canary,

00:28:46.620 --> 00:28:48.270
it would sing a song

00:28:48.270 --> 00:28:49.890
that was sort of like a hybrid in between.

00:28:49.890 --> 00:28:51.665
We call it a can-inch, right?

00:28:51.665 --> 00:28:53.460
[both laughing]

00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:55.290
And vice versa for the canary,

00:28:55.290 --> 00:28:56.340
because there's something different

00:28:56.340 --> 00:28:57.990
about their vocal musculature

00:28:57.990 --> 00:29:00.570
or the circuitry in the brain.

00:29:00.570 --> 00:29:03.780
And with a zebra finch, even
with a closely related species,

00:29:03.780 --> 00:29:07.320
if you would take a zebra
finch, a young animal,

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:10.590
and in one cage next to
it placed its own species,

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:12.150
adult male, right.

00:29:12.150 --> 00:29:15.510
And in the other cage placed
a Bengalese finch next to it,

00:29:15.510 --> 00:29:17.700
it would preferably learn the song

00:29:17.700 --> 00:29:20.400
from its own species neighbor.

00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:22.170
But if you remove its neighbor,

00:29:22.170 --> 00:29:24.900
it would learn that
Bengalese finch very well.

00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:26.400
- [Andrew] Fantastic.

00:29:26.400 --> 00:29:29.040
- It has something to do
with also the social bonding

00:29:29.040 --> 00:29:30.360
with your own species.

00:29:30.360 --> 00:29:31.590
- Incredible.

00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:33.450
That raises a question that I,

00:29:33.450 --> 00:29:34.830
based on something I also heard,

00:29:34.830 --> 00:29:37.260
but I don't have any scientific

00:29:37.260 --> 00:29:38.970
peer-reviewed publication to point to,

00:29:38.970 --> 00:29:41.490
which is this idea of Pidgin not the bird,

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:45.210
but this idea of when multiple cultures

00:29:45.210 --> 00:29:47.670
and languages converge in
a given geographic area,

00:29:47.670 --> 00:29:50.430
that the children of all the
different native languages

00:29:50.430 --> 00:29:53.220
will come up with their own language.

00:29:53.220 --> 00:29:55.020
I think this was in island culture,

00:29:55.020 --> 00:29:56.820
maybe in Hawaii, called Pidgin,

00:29:56.820 --> 00:29:59.580
which is sort of a hybrid
of the various languages

00:29:59.580 --> 00:30:01.227
that their parents speak at home

00:30:01.227 --> 00:30:03.390
and that they themselves speak.

00:30:03.390 --> 00:30:06.600
And that somehow Pidgin
again, not the bird,

00:30:06.600 --> 00:30:08.010
but a language called Pidgin,

00:30:08.010 --> 00:30:09.393
for reasons, I don't know,

00:30:10.410 --> 00:30:14.640
harbors certain basic
elements of all language.

00:30:14.640 --> 00:30:15.810
Is that true?

00:30:15.810 --> 00:30:17.481
Is that not true?

00:30:17.481 --> 00:30:19.410
- I would say, I haven't
studied enough myself

00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:21.510
in terms of Pidgin, specifically,

00:30:21.510 --> 00:30:24.150
but in terms of cultural
evolution of language

00:30:24.150 --> 00:30:27.570
and hybridization between
different cultures and so forth,

00:30:27.570 --> 00:30:30.540
even amongst birds with different dialects

00:30:30.540 --> 00:30:33.750
and you bring them together, you know,

00:30:33.750 --> 00:30:37.950
what is going on here
is cultural evolution

00:30:37.950 --> 00:30:41.190
remarkably tracks genetic evolution.

00:30:41.190 --> 00:30:43.260
So if you bring people

00:30:43.260 --> 00:30:45.090
from two separate populations together

00:30:45.090 --> 00:30:47.370
that have been in their
separate populations,

00:30:47.370 --> 00:30:51.360
evolutionarily, at least
for hundreds of generations,

00:30:51.360 --> 00:30:52.740
so someone's speaking Chinese,

00:30:52.740 --> 00:30:54.570
someone's speaking English,

00:30:54.570 --> 00:30:58.260
and that child is then
learning from both of them.

00:30:58.260 --> 00:31:00.690
Yes, that child's going
to be able to pick up

00:31:00.690 --> 00:31:05.690
and merge phonemes and words together

00:31:06.090 --> 00:31:09.210
in a way that an adult
wouldn't, because, why?

00:31:09.210 --> 00:31:12.930
They're experiencing both
languages at the same time

00:31:12.930 --> 00:31:16.050
during their critical period years,

00:31:16.050 --> 00:31:19.770
in a way that adults would
not be able to experience.

00:31:19.770 --> 00:31:21.570
And so you get a hybrid.

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:23.610
And the lowest common denominator

00:31:23.610 --> 00:31:25.380
is going to be what they share.

00:31:25.380 --> 00:31:27.840
And so the phonemes
that they've re retained

00:31:27.840 --> 00:31:31.830
in each of their languages
is what's going to be,

00:31:31.830 --> 00:31:33.600
I imagine, used the most.

00:31:33.600 --> 00:31:35.160
- Interesting.

00:31:35.160 --> 00:31:40.110
So we've got brain circuits
in songbirds and in humans

00:31:40.110 --> 00:31:41.730
that in many ways are similar,

00:31:41.730 --> 00:31:43.770
perhaps not in their exact wiring,

00:31:43.770 --> 00:31:45.960
but in their basic contour of wiring.

00:31:45.960 --> 00:31:47.430
And genes that are expressed

00:31:47.430 --> 00:31:51.480
in both sets of neural circuits
in very distinct species

00:31:51.480 --> 00:31:54.630
that are responsible for these phenomenon

00:31:54.630 --> 00:31:56.850
we're calling speech and language.

00:31:56.850 --> 00:32:00.570
What sorts of things are
those genes controlling?

00:32:00.570 --> 00:32:03.390
I could imagine they were
controlling the wiring

00:32:03.390 --> 00:32:04.980
of connections between brain areas.

00:32:04.980 --> 00:32:08.850
You know, essentially a map
of, you know, of a circuit,

00:32:08.850 --> 00:32:09.840
basically like an engineer

00:32:09.840 --> 00:32:11.610
would design a circuit
for speech and language,

00:32:11.610 --> 00:32:14.790
nature designed the circuit
for speech and language,

00:32:14.790 --> 00:32:16.770
but presumably other things too.

00:32:16.770 --> 00:32:21.360
Like the ability to connect motor patterns

00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:24.390
within the throat of
muscles within the throat,

00:32:24.390 --> 00:32:26.010
or in the control of the tongue.

00:32:26.010 --> 00:32:27.750
I mean, what are these genes doing?

00:32:27.750 --> 00:32:29.040
- You're pretty good, yeah.

00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:32.073
You've made some very good
guesses there that makes sense.

00:32:34.440 --> 00:32:36.810
So, yes, one of the things that differ

00:32:36.810 --> 00:32:38.280
in the speech pathways of us

00:32:38.280 --> 00:32:39.780
and these song pathways of birds

00:32:39.780 --> 00:32:42.420
is some of the connections
are fundamentally different

00:32:42.420 --> 00:32:44.040
than the surrounding circuits,

00:32:44.040 --> 00:32:47.910
like a direct cortical connection

00:32:47.910 --> 00:32:50.280
from the areas that control
vocalizations in the cortex

00:32:50.280 --> 00:32:52.560
or the motor neurons
that control the larynx,

00:32:52.560 --> 00:32:54.900
in humans or the syrinx in birds.

00:32:54.900 --> 00:32:57.630
And so we actually made a prediction

00:32:57.630 --> 00:33:00.090
that since some of these
connections differ,

00:33:00.090 --> 00:33:03.420
we're going to find genes that
control neural connectivity,

00:33:03.420 --> 00:33:05.850
and that specialize in
that function, that differ.

00:33:05.850 --> 00:33:07.623
And that's exactly what we found.

00:33:09.270 --> 00:33:11.550
Genes that control what
we call axon guidance

00:33:11.550 --> 00:33:12.870
and form neuronal connections,

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:14.070
and what was interesting,

00:33:14.070 --> 00:33:17.970
it was sort of in the opposite
direction that we expected.

00:33:17.970 --> 00:33:20.580
That is, some of these genes,

00:33:20.580 --> 00:33:21.600
actually, a number of them

00:33:21.600 --> 00:33:24.870
that control neural
connectivity were turned off

00:33:24.870 --> 00:33:27.870
in the speech circuit, all right.

00:33:27.870 --> 00:33:29.400
And it didn't make sense to us at first

00:33:29.400 --> 00:33:31.080
until we started to realize the function

00:33:31.080 --> 00:33:34.500
of these genes are to repel
connections from forming,

00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:36.390
so repulsive molecules.

00:33:36.390 --> 00:33:38.580
And so when you turn them off,

00:33:38.580 --> 00:33:40.260
they allow certain connections to form

00:33:40.260 --> 00:33:42.240
that normally would have not formed.

00:33:42.240 --> 00:33:43.890
So by turning it off,

00:33:43.890 --> 00:33:46.473
you gain a function for speech, right?

00:33:47.580 --> 00:33:49.680
Other genes that surprised us

00:33:49.680 --> 00:33:54.000
were genes involved in calcium
buffering neuroprotection,

00:33:54.000 --> 00:33:56.730
like Parvalbumin or heat-shock proteins,

00:33:56.730 --> 00:33:59.700
so when your brain gets
hot, these proteins turn on.

00:33:59.700 --> 00:34:01.320
And we couldn't figure
out for a long time,

00:34:01.320 --> 00:34:03.000
why is that the case?

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:06.510
And then the idea popped
to me one day and said, ah,

00:34:06.510 --> 00:34:09.900
when I heard the larynx is
the fastest firing muscles

00:34:09.900 --> 00:34:11.670
in the body, all right.

00:34:11.670 --> 00:34:14.100
In order to vibrate sound

00:34:14.100 --> 00:34:16.680
and modulate sound in the way we do,

00:34:16.680 --> 00:34:18.240
you have to control,

00:34:18.240 --> 00:34:20.880
you have to move those muscles, you know,

00:34:20.880 --> 00:34:23.100
three to four to five times faster

00:34:23.100 --> 00:34:25.470
than just regular walking or running.

00:34:25.470 --> 00:34:28.590
And so when you stick electrodes

00:34:28.590 --> 00:34:31.650
in the brain areas that
control learned vocalizations

00:34:31.650 --> 00:34:34.350
in these birds and I
think in humans as well,

00:34:34.350 --> 00:34:36.930
those neurons are firing at a higher rate

00:34:36.930 --> 00:34:38.790
to control these muscles.

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:40.950
And so what is that going to do?

00:34:40.950 --> 00:34:43.590
You're going to have lots of
toxicity in those neurons,

00:34:43.590 --> 00:34:45.450
unless you upregulate molecules

00:34:45.450 --> 00:34:48.743
that take out the extra load

00:34:48.743 --> 00:34:50.970
that is needed to control the larynx.

00:34:50.970 --> 00:34:52.860
And then finally, a third set of genes

00:34:52.860 --> 00:34:55.260
that are specialized
in these speech circuit

00:34:55.260 --> 00:34:56.943
are involved in neuroplasticity.

00:34:57.900 --> 00:35:01.590
Neuroplasticity, meaning
allowing the brain circuits

00:35:01.590 --> 00:35:05.670
to be more flexible so
you can learn better.

00:35:05.670 --> 00:35:06.823
And why is that?

00:35:06.823 --> 00:35:09.270
I think learning how to produce speech

00:35:09.270 --> 00:35:12.600
is a more complex learning ability

00:35:12.600 --> 00:35:14.580
than say learning how to walk

00:35:14.580 --> 00:35:17.340
or learning how to do tricks

00:35:17.340 --> 00:35:19.710
and jumps and so forth that dogs do.

00:35:19.710 --> 00:35:21.803
- Yeah, it's interesting as you say that,

00:35:21.803 --> 00:35:24.630
because I realize that
many aspects of speech

00:35:24.630 --> 00:35:25.710
are sort of reflexive.

00:35:25.710 --> 00:35:27.780
I'm not thinking about
each word I'm going to say,

00:35:27.780 --> 00:35:29.070
they just sort of roll out of my mouth,

00:35:29.070 --> 00:35:31.020
hopefully with some forethought.

00:35:31.020 --> 00:35:35.250
We both know people that
seem to speak, think less,

00:35:35.250 --> 00:35:37.350
fewer synapses between
their brain and their mouth

00:35:37.350 --> 00:35:38.340
than others, right.
- Yes.

00:35:38.340 --> 00:35:39.750
- A lot of examples out there,

00:35:39.750 --> 00:35:41.580
and some people are very
deliberate in their speech,

00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:46.410
but nonetheless, that much
of speech has to be precise.

00:35:46.410 --> 00:35:49.020
And some of it less precise.

00:35:49.020 --> 00:35:51.330
In terms of plasticity of speech

00:35:51.330 --> 00:35:53.700
and the ability to learn
multiple languages,

00:35:53.700 --> 00:35:55.680
but even just one language,

00:35:55.680 --> 00:35:57.870
what's going on in the critical period,

00:35:57.870 --> 00:35:59.190
the so-called critical period?
- Yeah.

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:02.970
- Why is it that, so my
niece speaks Spanish.

00:36:02.970 --> 00:36:04.440
She's Guatemalan and speaks Spanish

00:36:04.440 --> 00:36:06.240
and English incredibly well.

00:36:06.240 --> 00:36:08.190
She's 14-years-old.

00:36:08.190 --> 00:36:09.630
I've struggled with Spanish my whole life.

00:36:09.630 --> 00:36:10.590
My father is bilingual.

00:36:10.590 --> 00:36:11.700
My mother is not.

00:36:11.700 --> 00:36:13.980
I've tried to learn Spanish as an adult.

00:36:13.980 --> 00:36:15.930
It's really challenging.

00:36:15.930 --> 00:36:18.090
I'm told that had I learned
it when I was eight,

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:19.920
I would be better off.
- That's right.

00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:22.680
- Or it would be installed within me.

00:36:22.680 --> 00:36:24.510
So the first question is,

00:36:24.510 --> 00:36:26.370
is it easier to learn multiple languages

00:36:26.370 --> 00:36:28.080
without an accent early in life?

00:36:28.080 --> 00:36:29.250
And if so, why?

00:36:29.250 --> 00:36:30.780
And then the second question is

00:36:30.780 --> 00:36:34.560
if one can already speak
more than one language

00:36:34.560 --> 00:36:36.400
as a consequence of childhood learning,

00:36:36.400 --> 00:36:39.483
is it easier to acquire
new languages later on?

00:36:40.320 --> 00:36:43.443
- So, the answer to both of
those questions is yes, in that,

00:36:44.850 --> 00:36:49.020
but to explain this, I
need to let you know,

00:36:49.020 --> 00:36:51.390
actually the entire brain

00:36:51.390 --> 00:36:54.360
is undergoing a critical
period development,

00:36:54.360 --> 00:36:56.340
not just the speech pathways.

00:36:56.340 --> 00:36:59.280
And so it's easier to
learn how to play a piano.

00:36:59.280 --> 00:37:01.380
It's easier to learn how to ride a bike

00:37:01.380 --> 00:37:03.240
for the first time and so forth

00:37:03.240 --> 00:37:07.200
as a young child than it is later in life.

00:37:07.200 --> 00:37:11.550
What I mean easier in
terms of when you start

00:37:11.550 --> 00:37:13.920
from first principles
of learning something.

00:37:13.920 --> 00:37:15.510
So the very first time,

00:37:15.510 --> 00:37:17.070
if you're going to
learn Chinese as a child

00:37:17.070 --> 00:37:20.070
versus the very first time
you learn Chinese as an adult

00:37:20.070 --> 00:37:22.833
or learning to play piano
as a child versus an adult.

00:37:24.330 --> 00:37:26.490
But the speech pathways,

00:37:26.490 --> 00:37:28.320
or let's say speech behavior,

00:37:28.320 --> 00:37:31.950
I think has a stronger critical period

00:37:31.950 --> 00:37:34.290
change to it than other circuits.

00:37:34.290 --> 00:37:36.633
And why, what's going on there in general?

00:37:39.570 --> 00:37:41.010
Why do you need a critical period

00:37:41.010 --> 00:37:43.980
to make you more stable,

00:37:43.980 --> 00:37:47.160
to make you more stubborn, so to speak?

00:37:47.160 --> 00:37:52.107
The reason I believe is
that the brain is not for,

00:37:52.107 --> 00:37:55.560
the brain can only hold
so much information.

00:37:55.560 --> 00:38:00.560
And if you are undergoing
rapid learning to learn,

00:38:01.020 --> 00:38:02.670
to acquire new knowledge,

00:38:02.670 --> 00:38:05.760
you also have to, you know, dumb stuff.

00:38:05.760 --> 00:38:08.970
Put in memory or information in the trash,

00:38:08.970 --> 00:38:10.170
like in a computer.

00:38:10.170 --> 00:38:13.020
You only have so many gigabases of memory.

00:38:13.020 --> 00:38:17.280
And so therefore, plus also for survival,

00:38:17.280 --> 00:38:19.740
you don't want to keep forgetting things.

00:38:19.740 --> 00:38:23.850
And so the brain is designed, I believe,

00:38:23.850 --> 00:38:25.920
to undergo this critical period

00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:29.157
and solidify the circuits with
what you learned as a child

00:38:29.157 --> 00:38:31.140
and you use that for
the rest of your life.

00:38:31.140 --> 00:38:36.000
And we humans stay even more
plastic in our brain functions

00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:38.490
controlled by a gene called srGAP2.

00:38:38.490 --> 00:38:40.050
We have an extra copy of it

00:38:40.050 --> 00:38:42.240
that leads our speech circuit
and other brain regions

00:38:42.240 --> 00:38:44.550
in a more immature state throughout life

00:38:44.550 --> 00:38:46.080
compared to other animals.

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:47.730
So we're more immature.

00:38:47.730 --> 00:38:49.770
We're still juvenile like
compared to other animals.

00:38:49.770 --> 00:38:50.666
- I knew it.

00:38:50.666 --> 00:38:52.860
- But we still go through
the critical periods

00:38:52.860 --> 00:38:54.240
like they all do.

00:38:54.240 --> 00:38:57.120
And now the question you asked about,

00:38:57.120 --> 00:39:01.710
if you learn more languages as a child,

00:39:01.710 --> 00:39:03.600
is it easier to learn as an adult?

00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:06.390
And that's a common finding
out there in the literature.

00:39:06.390 --> 00:39:08.130
There's some that argue against it.

00:39:08.130 --> 00:39:10.893
But for those that support
it, the idea there is,

00:39:11.970 --> 00:39:14.640
you are born with a set of innate sounds

00:39:14.640 --> 00:39:16.740
you can produce of phonemes.

00:39:16.740 --> 00:39:18.000
And you narrow that down

00:39:18.000 --> 00:39:20.370
because not all languages use all of them.

00:39:20.370 --> 00:39:21.960
And so you narrow down the ones

00:39:21.960 --> 00:39:24.330
you use to string the phonemes together,

00:39:24.330 --> 00:39:26.130
in words that you learn

00:39:26.130 --> 00:39:29.460
and you maintain those
phonemes as an adult.

00:39:29.460 --> 00:39:31.650
And here comes along another language

00:39:31.650 --> 00:39:32.850
that's using those phonemes

00:39:32.850 --> 00:39:36.240
or in different combinations
you're not used to.

00:39:36.240 --> 00:39:39.480
And therefore, it's like
starting from first principles,

00:39:39.480 --> 00:39:41.070
but if you already have them

00:39:41.070 --> 00:39:43.230
in multiple languages that you're using,

00:39:43.230 --> 00:39:45.060
then it makes it easier to use them

00:39:45.060 --> 00:39:47.289
in another third or fourth language.

00:39:47.289 --> 00:39:49.980
- I see, incredible.

00:39:49.980 --> 00:39:52.216
- So, it's not like your brain

00:39:52.216 --> 00:39:54.570
has maintained greater plasticity,

00:39:54.570 --> 00:39:57.450
it's your brain has
maintained greater ability

00:39:57.450 --> 00:39:59.520
to produce different sounds

00:39:59.520 --> 00:40:02.160
that then allows you to learn
another language faster.

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:03.600
- Got it.

00:40:03.600 --> 00:40:08.010
Are the hand gestures
associated with sounds

00:40:08.010 --> 00:40:10.170
or with meanings of words?

00:40:10.170 --> 00:40:11.940
- I think the hand gestures are associated

00:40:11.940 --> 00:40:14.490
with both the sounds and the meaning.

00:40:14.490 --> 00:40:19.320
When I say sound like if
you are really angry, right,

00:40:19.320 --> 00:40:22.860
and you are making a loud
screaming noise, right,

00:40:22.860 --> 00:40:25.140
you may make hand gestures

00:40:25.140 --> 00:40:27.900
that look like you're going
to beat the wall, right?

00:40:27.900 --> 00:40:32.670
Because you're making loud
sounds and loud gestures, right.

00:40:32.670 --> 00:40:35.880
But if you want to explain
something like, come over here,

00:40:35.880 --> 00:40:38.640
what I just do now to you
for those who can't see me,

00:40:38.640 --> 00:40:41.880
I swung my hand towards you
and swung it here to me.

00:40:41.880 --> 00:40:44.460
That has a meaning to it, to come here.

00:40:44.460 --> 00:40:46.800
So just like with the voice,

00:40:46.800 --> 00:40:51.480
the hand gestures are
producing both, you know,

00:40:51.480 --> 00:40:53.130
both qualities of sound.

00:40:53.130 --> 00:40:55.890
- And for people that
speak multiple languages,

00:40:55.890 --> 00:40:58.410
especially those that learn
those multiple languages

00:40:58.410 --> 00:40:59.760
early in development,

00:40:59.760 --> 00:41:02.280
do they switch their
patterns of motor movements

00:41:02.280 --> 00:41:04.140
according to let's say,

00:41:04.140 --> 00:41:06.630
going from Italian to Arabic

00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:08.790
or from Arabic to French

00:41:08.790 --> 00:41:12.900
in a way that matches
the precision of language

00:41:12.900 --> 00:41:13.980
that they're speaking?

00:41:13.980 --> 00:41:14.813
- You know what?

00:41:14.813 --> 00:41:15.646
You just asked me a question,

00:41:15.646 --> 00:41:17.130
I don't know the answer to.

00:41:17.130 --> 00:41:21.133
I would imagine that would
make sense because of switching

00:41:23.190 --> 00:41:25.920
in terms of sometimes people
might call this code switching,

00:41:25.920 --> 00:41:28.410
even different dialects
of the same language.

00:41:28.410 --> 00:41:30.570
Could you do that with your gestures?

00:41:30.570 --> 00:41:34.296
I imagine so, but I really don't
know if that's true or not.

00:41:34.296 --> 00:41:35.910
- Okay, well, I certainly don't
know from my own experience

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:37.343
because I only speak one language.

00:41:37.343 --> 00:41:39.630
[both laughing]

