00:42:52.860 --> 00:42:57.540
To go a little bit into the
abstract, but not too far,

00:42:57.540 --> 00:42:59.430
what about modes of speech and language

00:42:59.430 --> 00:43:03.030
that seem to have a depth
of emotionality and meaning,

00:43:03.030 --> 00:43:06.990
but for which it departs
from structured language.

00:43:06.990 --> 00:43:09.990
Here's what I mean, poetry.
- Hmm hmm.

00:43:09.990 --> 00:43:11.400
- I think of musicians,

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:14.460
like there's some Bob
Dylan songs that, to me,

00:43:14.460 --> 00:43:17.190
I understand the individual words.

00:43:17.190 --> 00:43:20.250
I like to think there's an
emotion associated with it.

00:43:20.250 --> 00:43:21.960
at least, I experience
some sort of emotion

00:43:21.960 --> 00:43:25.650
and I have a guess about
what he was experiencing.

00:43:25.650 --> 00:43:29.490
But if I were to just read
it linearly without the music

00:43:29.490 --> 00:43:32.790
and without him singing it, or
somebody singing it like him,

00:43:32.790 --> 00:43:33.840
it wouldn't hold any meaning.

00:43:33.840 --> 00:43:37.200
So in other words, words
that seem to have meaning,

00:43:37.200 --> 00:43:39.660
but not associated with language,

00:43:39.660 --> 00:43:42.243
but somehow tap into an emotionality.

00:43:43.110 --> 00:43:44.283
- Yep, absolutely.

00:43:45.145 --> 00:43:49.200
So, we call this difference
semantic communication,

00:43:49.200 --> 00:43:51.150
communication with meaning

00:43:51.150 --> 00:43:52.590
and effective communication,

00:43:52.590 --> 00:43:54.750
communication that has more

00:43:54.750 --> 00:43:57.367
of an emotional feeling content to it,

00:43:57.367 --> 00:44:00.300
you know, but not with,
you know, the semantics.

00:44:00.300 --> 00:44:02.580
And the two can be mixed up,

00:44:02.580 --> 00:44:05.700
like with singing words that have meaning,

00:44:05.700 --> 00:44:07.950
but also have this effect of emotional,

00:44:07.950 --> 00:44:12.030
you just love the sound of the
singer that you're hearing.

00:44:12.030 --> 00:44:16.740
And initially, you know,

00:44:16.740 --> 00:44:18.780
psychologists, scientists, in general,

00:44:18.780 --> 00:44:20.580
thought that these were
going to be controlled

00:44:20.580 --> 00:44:22.113
by different brain circuits.

00:44:23.040 --> 00:44:24.330
And it is the case.

00:44:24.330 --> 00:44:27.120
There are emotional brain
centers in the hypothalamus,

00:44:27.120 --> 00:44:29.700
in the cingulate cortex and so forth,

00:44:29.700 --> 00:44:33.060
that do give tone to the sounds.

00:44:33.060 --> 00:44:37.140
But I believe, you know,
based upon imaging work

00:44:37.140 --> 00:44:39.570
and work we see in birds,

00:44:39.570 --> 00:44:42.720
when birds are communicating
semantic information

00:44:42.720 --> 00:44:45.630
in their sounds, which is not
too often, but it happens,

00:44:45.630 --> 00:44:48.510
versus effective communication,

00:44:48.510 --> 00:44:50.550
sing because I'm trying
to attract the mate,

00:44:50.550 --> 00:44:53.370
my courtship song or defend my territory,

00:44:53.370 --> 00:44:54.690
it's the same brain circuits.

00:44:54.690 --> 00:44:56.580
It's the same speech-like or song,

00:44:56.580 --> 00:44:59.253
circuits are being used in different ways.

00:45:00.945 --> 00:45:03.990
- A friend of mine, who's
also a therapist, said to me,

00:45:03.990 --> 00:45:05.220
you know, it's possible to say,

00:45:05.220 --> 00:45:07.920
I love you with intense hatred

00:45:07.920 --> 00:45:10.200
than to say, I hate you with intense love.

00:45:10.200 --> 00:45:11.033
- [Eric] Right.

00:45:11.033 --> 00:45:12.540
- And reminding me that
it's possible to hear

00:45:12.540 --> 00:45:15.060
both of those statements in either way.

00:45:15.060 --> 00:45:19.290
So I guess it's not just
limited to song or poetry.

00:45:19.290 --> 00:45:23.700
It also, there's something
about the intention

00:45:23.700 --> 00:45:27.240
and the emotional context
in which something spoken

00:45:27.240 --> 00:45:29.070
that it can heavily shape the way

00:45:29.070 --> 00:45:31.260
that we interpret what we hear.

00:45:31.260 --> 00:45:32.310
- That's right.

00:45:32.310 --> 00:45:35.430
And I consider all of
that actually, meaning,

00:45:35.430 --> 00:45:37.710
even though I defined it as,

00:45:37.710 --> 00:45:41.730
people commonly do semantic
and effective communication.

00:45:41.730 --> 00:45:43.980
Effective communication
to say, I hate you,

00:45:43.980 --> 00:45:45.753
but meant love, right,

00:45:47.490 --> 00:45:51.330
does have emotional
meaning to it, you know?

00:45:51.330 --> 00:45:53.760
And so, you know, one's more
like an object kind of meaning

00:45:53.760 --> 00:45:55.710
or an abstract kind of meaning.

00:45:55.710 --> 00:45:56.790
There's several other points here

00:45:56.790 --> 00:46:00.450
I think it's important for those
listening out there to hear

00:46:00.450 --> 00:46:02.340
is that when I say also

00:46:02.340 --> 00:46:06.010
this effective and semantic communication

00:46:07.320 --> 00:46:08.850
being used by similar brain circuits,

00:46:08.850 --> 00:46:11.310
it also matters, the side of the brain.

00:46:11.310 --> 00:46:13.623
In birds and in humans,

00:46:14.490 --> 00:46:17.340
there's left-right dominance

00:46:17.340 --> 00:46:21.510
for learned communication,
learned sound communication.

00:46:21.510 --> 00:46:25.710
So the left in us humans is
more dominant for speech,

00:46:25.710 --> 00:46:29.250
but the right has a
more balance for singing

00:46:29.250 --> 00:46:31.170
or processing musical sounds

00:46:31.170 --> 00:46:32.850
as opposed to processing speech.

00:46:32.850 --> 00:46:34.860
Both get used for both reasons.

00:46:34.860 --> 00:46:37.590
And so when people say your right brain

00:46:37.590 --> 00:46:39.120
is your artistic brain

00:46:39.120 --> 00:46:41.430
and your left brain is
your thinking brain,

00:46:41.430 --> 00:46:43.710
this is what they're referring to.

00:46:43.710 --> 00:46:46.260
And so that's another distinction.

00:46:46.260 --> 00:46:49.610
A second thing that's useful to know

00:46:49.610 --> 00:46:51.720
is that all vocal learning species

00:46:51.720 --> 00:46:55.380
use their learned sounds
for this emotional

00:46:55.380 --> 00:46:58.050
effective kind of communication,

00:46:58.050 --> 00:47:00.390
but only a few of them like humans

00:47:00.390 --> 00:47:02.190
and some parrots and dolphins

00:47:02.190 --> 00:47:05.070
use it for the semantic
kind of communication,

00:47:05.070 --> 00:47:06.213
we're calling speech.

00:47:07.230 --> 00:47:11.280
And that has led a number
of people to hypothesize

00:47:11.280 --> 00:47:14.430
that the evolution of
spoken language, of speech,

00:47:14.430 --> 00:47:17.190
evolved first for singing,

00:47:17.190 --> 00:47:19.710
for this more like emotional

00:47:19.710 --> 00:47:22.560
kind of mate attraction
like the Jennifer Lopez,

00:47:22.560 --> 00:47:24.903
the Ricky Martin kind
of songs and so forth.

00:47:25.740 --> 00:47:27.450
And then later on,

00:47:27.450 --> 00:47:29.610
it became used for abstract communication

00:47:29.610 --> 00:47:31.050
like we're doing now.

00:47:31.050 --> 00:47:32.190
- Oh, interesting.

00:47:32.190 --> 00:47:34.680
Well, that's a perfect segue for me

00:47:34.680 --> 00:47:38.400
to be able to ask you
about your background

00:47:38.400 --> 00:47:42.720
and motor control, not only
of the hands but of the body.

00:47:42.720 --> 00:47:46.350
So you have a number of important
distinctions to your name,

00:47:46.350 --> 00:47:49.110
but one of them is that you were a member

00:47:49.110 --> 00:47:52.110
of the Alvin Ailey Dance School,

00:47:52.110 --> 00:47:52.980
School of Dance.
- That's right.

00:47:52.980 --> 00:47:54.030
That's right, hmm hmm.

00:47:54.030 --> 00:47:55.680
- So you're an accomplished

00:47:55.680 --> 00:47:58.170
and quite able dancer, right?

00:47:58.170 --> 00:48:00.840
Tell us a little bit about your background

00:48:00.840 --> 00:48:03.120
in the world of dance

00:48:03.120 --> 00:48:07.389
and how it informs your
interest in neuroscience,

00:48:07.389 --> 00:48:08.222
[clears throat], excuse me,

00:48:08.222 --> 00:48:09.960
and perhaps even how it relates

00:48:09.960 --> 00:48:12.090
specifically to your work
on speech and language.

00:48:12.090 --> 00:48:14.490
- Yes, well, it's interesting.

00:48:14.490 --> 00:48:17.310
And then this kind of history
even goes before my time.

00:48:17.310 --> 00:48:19.920
So in my family, my
mother and father's side,

00:48:19.920 --> 00:48:21.750
they both went to the High
School of Music and Art

00:48:21.750 --> 00:48:23.580
here in New York City.

00:48:23.580 --> 00:48:25.230
And particularly, in my mother's family,

00:48:25.230 --> 00:48:28.410
going back multiple
generations, they were singers.

00:48:28.410 --> 00:48:30.330
And I even did my family genealogy

00:48:30.330 --> 00:48:32.610
and found out not only, you know,

00:48:32.610 --> 00:48:35.460
we have some relationships
to some well-known singers,

00:48:35.460 --> 00:48:37.800
distant relationships
like Thelonious Monk,

00:48:37.800 --> 00:48:40.410
but going back to the plantations

00:48:40.410 --> 00:48:42.930
in North Carolina and so forth,

00:48:42.930 --> 00:48:45.810
my ancestors were singers in the church

00:48:45.810 --> 00:48:47.970
for the, you know, the towns and so forth.

00:48:47.970 --> 00:48:49.800
And this somehow got passed on

00:48:49.800 --> 00:48:51.930
multiple generations to my family.

00:48:51.930 --> 00:48:53.700
And I thought I was going to grow up

00:48:53.700 --> 00:48:55.260
and be a famous singer, right.

00:48:55.260 --> 00:48:57.990
And me and my brothers and sister

00:48:57.990 --> 00:49:01.680
formed a band when we
were kids and so forth.

00:49:01.680 --> 00:49:04.620
But it turned out that I didn't inherit

00:49:04.620 --> 00:49:07.620
the singing talents of some
of my other family members,

00:49:07.620 --> 00:49:10.200
even though, you know,
I was, you know, okay.

00:49:10.200 --> 00:49:11.970
You know, but not like my brother,

00:49:11.970 --> 00:49:16.170
or not like my mother or my
aunts and my cousin Pura Fe',

00:49:16.170 --> 00:49:18.990
who's now a talented
Native American singer.

00:49:18.990 --> 00:49:21.603
And so,

00:49:23.880 --> 00:49:27.300
that then influenced
me to do other things.

00:49:27.300 --> 00:49:32.040
And I started, you know,
competing in dance contests,

00:49:32.040 --> 00:49:33.450
you know, actually this is around the time

00:49:33.450 --> 00:49:36.150
of Saturday Night Fever
and I was as a teenager.

00:49:36.150 --> 00:49:38.400
And I started winning dance contests.

00:49:38.400 --> 00:49:40.110
And I thought, oh, I can dance.

00:49:40.110 --> 00:49:42.870
And I auditioned for the High
School of Performing Arts.

00:49:42.870 --> 00:49:45.390
And I got in, here in New York City,

00:49:45.390 --> 00:49:48.150
and got into ballet
dance and got in, right.

00:49:48.150 --> 00:49:49.830
And thought, if I learned ballet,

00:49:49.830 --> 00:49:50.880
I can learn everything else.

00:49:50.880 --> 00:49:53.010
It I that idea, if you
learn something classical,

00:49:53.010 --> 00:49:55.650
it can teach for everything else.

00:49:55.650 --> 00:49:58.320
And I was, yeah, at
Alvin Ailey Dance School,

00:49:58.320 --> 00:50:00.060
Joffrey Ballet Dance School.

00:50:00.060 --> 00:50:03.453
And at the end of my senior concert,

00:50:04.290 --> 00:50:06.300
I had this opportunity to audition

00:50:06.300 --> 00:50:08.370
for the Alvin Ailey Dance Company.

00:50:08.370 --> 00:50:11.070
And I had an opportunity to go to college.

00:50:11.070 --> 00:50:13.260
And I also fell in love
with another passion

00:50:13.260 --> 00:50:15.840
that my father had, which was science.

00:50:15.840 --> 00:50:18.510
And so I liked science in high school.

00:50:18.510 --> 00:50:22.650
And I found an overlap also
between the arts and sciences,

00:50:22.650 --> 00:50:26.490
you know, both required
creativity, hard work, discipline,

00:50:26.490 --> 00:50:29.700
you know, new discovery,
both weren't boring to me.

00:50:29.700 --> 00:50:33.810
And the one decision I made
at that senior dance concert

00:50:33.810 --> 00:50:36.780
was, you know, when talking
to the Alvin Ailey recruiter

00:50:36.780 --> 00:50:37.613
and thinking about it,

00:50:37.613 --> 00:50:39.360
I have to make a decision.

00:50:39.360 --> 00:50:42.720
And I thought something
my mother taught me

00:50:42.720 --> 00:50:45.967
because she was growing up in
the 1960s cultural revolution,

00:50:45.967 --> 00:50:49.920
"Do something that has a
positive impact on society."

00:50:49.920 --> 00:50:51.300
And I thought that I could do that better

00:50:51.300 --> 00:50:53.370
as a dancer than a scientist.

00:50:53.370 --> 00:50:57.810
So now jump, I get into
college, undergraduate school,

00:50:57.810 --> 00:51:00.120
I major in molecular
biology and mathematics.

00:51:00.120 --> 00:51:02.070
I decide I want to be a biologist,

00:51:02.070 --> 00:51:03.150
got into graduate school,

00:51:03.150 --> 00:51:04.817
wanted to study the brain at, you know,

00:51:04.817 --> 00:51:06.420
at the Rockefeller University.

00:51:06.420 --> 00:51:09.027
So I went from Hunter College
to Rockefeller University.

00:51:09.027 --> 00:51:11.429
And so now I got to the brain

00:51:11.429 --> 00:51:14.130
and why did I choose the brain

00:51:14.130 --> 00:51:17.580
is because it controls dancing. [laughs]

00:51:17.580 --> 00:51:19.920
But there wasn't anybody studying dancing.

00:51:19.920 --> 00:51:21.270
And I wanted study the brain,

00:51:21.270 --> 00:51:22.530
something that it does

00:51:22.530 --> 00:51:24.990
that's really interesting and complex.

00:51:24.990 --> 00:51:27.480
And I thought, ah,
language is what it does.

00:51:27.480 --> 00:51:28.650
You couldn't study that in mice.

00:51:28.650 --> 00:51:30.690
You couldn't study in non-human primates.

00:51:30.690 --> 00:51:32.430
But these birds do this wonderful thing

00:51:32.430 --> 00:51:34.890
that Fernando Nottebohm was
studying at Rockefeller.

00:51:34.890 --> 00:51:37.083
And so that's what got me into the birds.

00:51:40.459 --> 00:51:43.290
And then jumping now, 15 years later,

00:51:43.290 --> 00:51:44.880
you know, yeah, that's right.

00:51:44.880 --> 00:51:48.090
Even after I'm into now having my own lab,

00:51:48.090 --> 00:51:50.460
studying vocal learning in these birds

00:51:50.460 --> 00:51:52.440
as a model for language and humans,

00:51:52.440 --> 00:51:55.050
it turns out that, you know,

00:51:55.050 --> 00:51:58.110
Ani Patel and, you know, others,

00:51:58.110 --> 00:52:02.490
have discovered that only
vocal learning species

00:52:02.490 --> 00:52:03.873
can learn how to dance.

00:52:04.716 --> 00:52:07.410
- Is that right?
- That's right, yes.

00:52:07.410 --> 00:52:09.570
- So I've seen these just scrolling

00:52:09.570 --> 00:52:12.150
through the files here in my mind.

00:52:12.150 --> 00:52:13.920
I think about, every once
in a while someone will,

00:52:13.920 --> 00:52:16.110
I love parrots.
- Yes.

00:52:16.110 --> 00:52:16.943
- So every once in a while,

00:52:16.943 --> 00:52:19.200
someone will send me one
of these little Instagram

00:52:19.200 --> 00:52:20.760
or Twitter videos of a parrot

00:52:20.760 --> 00:52:22.470
doing what looks to me like dance,

00:52:22.470 --> 00:52:24.090
typically it's a cockatoo.
- That's right.

00:52:24.090 --> 00:52:24.923
- Right.
- That's right.

00:52:24.923 --> 00:52:27.000
- Even foot stomping to the sound and-

00:52:27.000 --> 00:52:28.530
- Famous one called Snowball out there,

00:52:28.530 --> 00:52:31.647
but there are many Snowballs
out there. [laughs]

00:52:31.647 --> 00:52:33.630
- All the dancing birds
are named Snowball?

00:52:33.630 --> 00:52:35.080
That's an interesting tactic.

00:52:36.000 --> 00:52:40.020
So only animals with language dance?

00:52:40.020 --> 00:52:41.700
- Yeah, vocal learning in particular,

00:52:41.700 --> 00:52:44.550
the ability to imitate sounds, yes.

00:52:44.550 --> 00:52:45.627
- Incredible.
- Yes.

00:52:45.627 --> 00:52:49.623
And this now is bringing
my life full circle, right.

00:52:50.550 --> 00:52:54.063
And so when that was discovered in 2009,

00:52:55.170 --> 00:52:57.870
at that same time in my lab at Duke,

00:52:57.870 --> 00:53:02.100
we had discovered that vocal
learning brain pathways

00:53:02.100 --> 00:53:04.560
in songbirds, as well as in humans

00:53:04.560 --> 00:53:07.143
and in parrots, right, like Snowball,

00:53:08.010 --> 00:53:09.510
are embedded within circuits

00:53:09.510 --> 00:53:11.253
that control learning how to move.

00:53:12.540 --> 00:53:15.660
And that led us to a theory
called the Brain Pathway

00:53:15.660 --> 00:53:18.270
or Motor Theory of Vocal Learning Origin

00:53:18.270 --> 00:53:21.330
where the brain pathways for
vocal learning and speech

00:53:21.330 --> 00:53:23.460
evolved by a whole duplication

00:53:23.460 --> 00:53:24.930
of the surrounding motor circuits

00:53:24.930 --> 00:53:27.630
involving learning how to move.

00:53:27.630 --> 00:53:30.750
Now, how does that explain dance, right?

00:53:30.750 --> 00:53:35.250
Well, when Snowball, the
cockatoos, are dancing,

00:53:35.250 --> 00:53:36.690
they're using the brain regions

00:53:36.690 --> 00:53:38.880
around their speech-like circuits

00:53:38.880 --> 00:53:40.800
to do this dancing behavior.

00:53:40.800 --> 00:53:42.360
And so what's going on there?

00:53:42.360 --> 00:53:46.500
What we hypothesize and now like to test

00:53:46.500 --> 00:53:49.230
is that when this,

00:53:49.230 --> 00:53:51.960
when speech evolved in humans

00:53:51.960 --> 00:53:55.230
and the equivalent behavior
and parrots and songbirds,

00:53:55.230 --> 00:53:58.980
it required a very tight integration

00:53:58.980 --> 00:54:01.890
in the brain regions that can hear sound

00:54:01.890 --> 00:54:04.470
with the brain regions
that control your muscles

00:54:04.470 --> 00:54:07.260
from moving your larynx
and tongue and so forth

00:54:07.260 --> 00:54:08.493
for producing sound.

00:54:09.359 --> 00:54:11.880
And that tight auditory motor integration,

00:54:11.880 --> 00:54:15.957
we argue, then contaminated
the surrounding brain regions.

00:54:15.957 --> 00:54:18.780
And that contamination of
the surrounding brain regions

00:54:18.780 --> 00:54:22.740
now allows us humans, in
particular, and parrots.

00:54:22.740 --> 00:54:26.310
to coordinate our muscle
movements of the rest of the body

00:54:26.310 --> 00:54:30.690
with sound in the same way
we do for speech sounds.

00:54:30.690 --> 00:54:31.523
- [Andrew] Well.

00:54:31.523 --> 00:54:33.540
- So we're speaking with
our bodies when we dance.

00:54:33.540 --> 00:54:34.497
- Incredible.

00:54:34.497 --> 00:54:36.150
And I have to say that

00:54:36.150 --> 00:54:38.760
as poor as I am at speaking
multiple languages,

00:54:38.760 --> 00:54:41.310
I'm even worse at dancing, so.

00:54:41.310 --> 00:54:44.190
- But I guarantee you're
better than a monkey.

00:54:44.190 --> 00:54:45.600
- But not Snowball, the cockatoo?

00:54:45.600 --> 00:54:47.550
- No, maybe not Snowball.

00:54:47.550 --> 00:54:48.870
On YouTube, we have a video

00:54:48.870 --> 00:54:51.210
where there's some scientists
dancing with Snowball

00:54:51.210 --> 00:54:52.950
and you'll see Snowballs doing better

00:54:52.950 --> 00:54:54.279
than some of the scientists.

00:54:54.279 --> 00:54:55.530
- Okay, well, as long as I'm not the worst

00:54:55.530 --> 00:54:57.683
of all scientists dancing.
- I don't think so.

00:54:58.710 --> 00:55:00.510
- There's always a neuroplasticity.

00:55:00.510 --> 00:55:02.103
May it save me someday.

00:55:03.300 --> 00:55:04.980
You said something incredible

00:55:04.980 --> 00:55:09.770
that I completely believe
even though I have minimum to,

00:55:10.650 --> 00:55:12.720
let's just say minimum dancing ability.

00:55:12.720 --> 00:55:14.640
Okay, I can get by at a party or wedding

00:55:14.640 --> 00:55:16.290
without complete embarrassment,

00:55:16.290 --> 00:55:18.933
but I don't have any structured training.

00:55:20.040 --> 00:55:25.040
So the body clearly can
communicate with movement.

00:55:26.880 --> 00:55:29.673
As a trained dancer and
knowing other trained dancers,

00:55:30.979 --> 00:55:34.830
I always think of dance
and bodily movement

00:55:34.830 --> 00:55:36.600
and communication through bodily movement

00:55:36.600 --> 00:55:38.640
as a form of wordlessness,

00:55:38.640 --> 00:55:40.230
like a state of wordlessness.

00:55:40.230 --> 00:55:42.000
In fact, the few times when I think

00:55:42.000 --> 00:55:45.840
that maybe I'm actually
dancing modestly well

00:55:45.840 --> 00:55:47.520
for the context that I'm in,

00:55:47.520 --> 00:55:48.960
or I see other people dancing

00:55:48.960 --> 00:55:51.420
and they seem to just be
very much in the movement,

00:55:51.420 --> 00:55:54.420
it's almost like a state of non-language,

00:55:54.420 --> 00:55:56.347
non-spoken language.
- Hmm hmm.

00:55:57.591 --> 00:55:59.790
- And yet what you're telling me is

00:55:59.790 --> 00:56:02.700
that there's a direct bridge at some level

00:56:02.700 --> 00:56:05.670
between the movement of
the body and language.

00:56:05.670 --> 00:56:08.550
So is there a language of the body

00:56:08.550 --> 00:56:12.030
that is distinct from
the language of speech?

00:56:12.030 --> 00:56:15.810
And if so, or if not, how do
those map onto one another?

00:56:15.810 --> 00:56:17.460
What does that Venn diagram look like?

00:56:17.460 --> 00:56:18.423
- Yeah, yeah.

00:56:19.680 --> 00:56:22.380
So, let me define first
dance in this context

00:56:22.380 --> 00:56:24.600
of vocal learning species.

00:56:24.600 --> 00:56:26.130
This is the kind of dancing

00:56:26.130 --> 00:56:28.410
that we are specialized in doing

00:56:28.410 --> 00:56:31.380
and the vocal learning species
are specialized in doing

00:56:31.380 --> 00:56:34.560
is synchronizing body movements of muscles

00:56:34.560 --> 00:56:37.050
to the rhythmic beats of music.

00:56:37.050 --> 00:56:39.570
And for some reason, we like doing that.

00:56:39.570 --> 00:56:41.770
We like synchronizing to sound

00:56:42.719 --> 00:56:45.390
and doing it together
as a group of people.

00:56:45.390 --> 00:56:49.110
And that kind of communication
amongst ourselves

00:56:49.110 --> 00:56:51.810
is more like the effective
kind of communication

00:56:51.810 --> 00:56:53.040
I mentioned earlier,

00:56:53.040 --> 00:56:54.900
unlike the semantic kind.

00:56:54.900 --> 00:56:58.500
So we, humans, are using our voices more

00:56:58.500 --> 00:57:02.460
for the semantic, abstract communication,

00:57:02.460 --> 00:57:04.470
but we're using learned dance

00:57:04.470 --> 00:57:09.210
for the effective emotional
bonding kind of communication.

00:57:09.210 --> 00:57:12.240
It doesn't mean we can't
communicate semantic information

00:57:12.240 --> 00:57:13.623
in dance, and we do it,

00:57:14.490 --> 00:57:16.440
but it's not as popular.

00:57:16.440 --> 00:57:19.770
You know, like a ballet that,
you know, in the Nutcracker,

00:57:19.770 --> 00:57:21.180
it is popular, you know,

00:57:21.180 --> 00:57:23.010
where they are communicating,

00:57:23.010 --> 00:57:25.980
you know, the Arabian guy comes out,

00:57:25.980 --> 00:57:28.200
which I was the Arabian guy
in the ballet Nutcracker.

00:57:28.200 --> 00:57:29.033
That's how I remember.
- Oh, yeah?

00:57:29.033 --> 00:57:30.780
- Yeah, for the
Westchester Ballet Company,

00:57:30.780 --> 00:57:31.923
when I was a teenager.

00:57:33.330 --> 00:57:35.700
You know, we're trying
to communicate meaning

00:57:35.700 --> 00:57:37.350
and our ballet dancing, it can go on

00:57:37.350 --> 00:57:39.630
with a whole story and so forth.

00:57:39.630 --> 00:57:42.720
But people don't interpret
that as clearly as speech.

00:57:42.720 --> 00:57:44.610
You know, they're seeing the ballet

00:57:44.610 --> 00:57:46.200
with semantic communication,

00:57:46.200 --> 00:57:48.570
with a lot of emotional content,

00:57:48.570 --> 00:57:51.000
whereas you go out to a club, you know,

00:57:51.000 --> 00:57:53.910
yeah, you're not communicating,

00:57:53.910 --> 00:57:55.170
okay, how you're feeling today?

00:57:55.170 --> 00:57:57.330
Tell me about your day and so forth.

00:57:57.330 --> 00:57:59.970
You're trying to synchronize
with other people

00:57:59.970 --> 00:58:01.350
in an effective way.

00:58:01.350 --> 00:58:03.213
And I think that's because,

00:58:04.440 --> 00:58:06.960
the dance brain circuit

00:58:06.960 --> 00:58:10.080
inherited the more ancient
part of the speech circuit,

00:58:10.080 --> 00:58:12.150
which was for singing.

00:58:12.150 --> 00:58:13.230
- I always had the feeling

00:58:13.230 --> 00:58:17.160
that with certain forms of
music, in particular opera,

00:58:17.160 --> 00:58:22.160
but any kind of music where
there's some long notes sung

00:58:23.340 --> 00:58:25.830
that at some level,

00:58:25.830 --> 00:58:29.220
there was a literal resonance created

00:58:29.220 --> 00:58:31.263
between the singer and the listener.

00:58:33.153 --> 00:58:35.970
Or I think of like the
deep voice of a Johnny Cash

00:58:35.970 --> 00:58:37.410
or where at some level,

00:58:37.410 --> 00:58:40.380
you can almost feel the
voice in your own body.

00:58:40.380 --> 00:58:45.380
And in theory, that could
be the vibration of the

00:58:45.570 --> 00:58:46.950
or the firing of the phrenic nerve

00:58:46.950 --> 00:58:49.040
controlling the diaphragm for all I know.

00:58:49.040 --> 00:58:51.120
Is there any evidence that
there's a coordination

00:58:51.120 --> 00:58:53.693
between performer and audience

00:58:53.693 --> 00:58:57.123
at the level of mind and body?

00:59:00.720 --> 00:59:04.110
- I'm going to say, possibly, yes.

00:59:04.110 --> 00:59:05.220
And the reason why is

00:59:05.220 --> 00:59:07.350
because I just came back from a conference

00:59:07.350 --> 00:59:08.973
on the neurobiology of dance-

00:59:10.470 --> 00:59:12.153
- Clearly, I'm going
to the wrong meetings.

00:59:12.153 --> 00:59:13.506
- Yeah, a colleague invited me.

00:59:13.506 --> 00:59:14.760
- You know, vision science
sounds be so boring.

00:59:14.760 --> 00:59:16.710
- Yes, well, one of my colleagues,

00:59:16.710 --> 00:59:18.840
Tecumseh Fitch and Jonathan Fritz,

00:59:18.840 --> 00:59:21.600
they organized, well, a particular section

00:59:21.600 --> 00:59:23.790
on this conference in Virginia.

00:59:23.790 --> 00:59:25.890
And this is the first
time I was in the room

00:59:25.890 --> 00:59:28.170
with so many neuroscientists

00:59:28.170 --> 00:59:29.760
studying the neurobiology of dance.

00:59:29.760 --> 00:59:32.580
It's a new field now,
in the last five years.

00:59:32.580 --> 00:59:36.900
And there was one lab

00:59:36.900 --> 00:59:41.610
where they were putting EEG
electrodes on the dancers,

00:59:41.610 --> 00:59:44.490
on two different dancers
partnering with each other,

00:59:44.490 --> 00:59:49.380
as well as the audience,
you know, seeing the dance

00:59:49.380 --> 00:59:50.910
and some, you know, argued,

00:59:50.910 --> 00:59:52.283
okay, if you're listening
to the music as well,

00:59:52.283 --> 00:59:53.580
how are you responding

00:59:53.580 --> 00:59:55.680
'cause you're asking
a question about music

00:59:55.680 --> 00:59:58.080
and I'm giving you an answer about dance.

00:59:58.080 --> 01:00:02.310
And what they found is
that, you know, the dancers,

01:00:02.310 --> 01:00:04.650
when they resonated with
each other during the dance,

01:00:04.650 --> 01:00:07.800
or the audience listening to
the dancers and the music,

01:00:07.800 --> 01:00:09.690
there's some resonance going on there

01:00:09.690 --> 01:00:13.110
that they've score as higher resonance.

01:00:13.110 --> 01:00:16.470
Their brain activity with
these wireless EEG signals

01:00:16.470 --> 01:00:18.090
are showing something different.

01:00:18.090 --> 01:00:20.700
And so that's why I say possibly, yes.

01:00:20.700 --> 01:00:22.360
It needs more rigorous study

01:00:23.220 --> 01:00:25.710
and you know, this is
some stuff they publish,

01:00:25.710 --> 01:00:27.540
but it's not prime time yet,

01:00:27.540 --> 01:00:29.400
but they're trying to figure this out.

01:00:29.400 --> 01:00:30.233
- Love it.

01:00:30.233 --> 01:00:32.310
So at least if I can't dance well,

01:00:32.310 --> 01:00:34.440
maybe I can hear and feel

01:00:34.440 --> 01:00:36.213
what it is to dance in a certain way.

01:00:36.213 --> 01:00:37.650
- Yes, that's right.

01:00:37.650 --> 01:00:41.610
And this will be, some
people will think that they,

01:00:41.610 --> 01:00:43.770
even songs that they hear

01:00:43.770 --> 01:00:46.890
and they can almost sing to
themselves in their own head

01:00:46.890 --> 01:00:49.163
and they know what they
want it to sound like.

01:00:49.163 --> 01:00:51.270
And you know when it really sounds good,

01:00:51.270 --> 01:00:52.590
what it sounds like,

01:00:52.590 --> 01:00:55.500
but they can't get their voice to do it.

01:00:55.500 --> 01:00:56.760
- I'm raising, for those listening,

01:00:56.760 --> 01:00:58.290
I'm raising my hand.

01:00:58.290 --> 01:01:00.120
No musical ability.

01:01:00.120 --> 01:01:03.300
Others in my household have
tremendous musical ability

01:01:03.300 --> 01:01:06.210
with instruments and
with voice, but not me.

01:01:06.210 --> 01:01:11.210
- Yeah, well, and so this
is one of my selfish goals

01:01:11.790 --> 01:01:13.770
of trying to find the genetics

01:01:13.770 --> 01:01:17.760
of why can some people who
sing really well and some not.

01:01:17.760 --> 01:01:20.547
Is there some genetic
predisposition to that?

01:01:20.547 --> 01:01:22.890
And then can I modify my own muscles

01:01:22.890 --> 01:01:24.450
of brain circuits to sing better?

01:01:24.450 --> 01:01:26.244
- You're still after the sing.

01:01:26.244 --> 01:01:27.077
I guess this is what happens

01:01:27.077 --> 01:01:30.750
when siblings vary in proficiency

01:01:30.750 --> 01:01:33.510
is that competitiveness amongst brothers

01:01:33.510 --> 01:01:34.830
and sisters never goes away.

01:01:34.830 --> 01:01:36.780
- I've been trying to be
as good as my brother,

01:01:36.780 --> 01:01:40.350
Mark and Victor, you
know, for my entire life.

01:01:40.350 --> 01:01:41.430
- Well, watch out, Mark and Victor,

01:01:41.430 --> 01:01:45.030
he's coming for you with
neuroscience to back him.

01:01:45.030 --> 01:01:49.410
Earlier, you said that you
discovered that you could dance.

01:01:49.410 --> 01:01:51.360
That caught my ear.

01:01:51.360 --> 01:01:52.980
It sounds like you
didn't actually have to,

01:01:52.980 --> 01:01:55.047
I'm not suggesting you
didn't work hard at it.

01:01:55.047 --> 01:01:57.600
But at the moment where you discovered it,

01:01:57.600 --> 01:02:00.270
it just sort of was a skill that you had,

01:02:00.270 --> 01:02:01.920
that up until that point,

01:02:01.920 --> 01:02:06.150
you didn't target a life
in the world of dance,

01:02:06.150 --> 01:02:08.077
but the fact that you quote, unquote,

01:02:08.077 --> 01:02:10.170
"discovered that you
could dance really well"

01:02:10.170 --> 01:02:11.940
and then went to this
incredible school of dance

01:02:11.940 --> 01:02:13.530
and did well,

01:02:13.530 --> 01:02:16.410
tells me that perhaps there is an ability

01:02:16.410 --> 01:02:18.780
that was built up in childhood

01:02:18.780 --> 01:02:21.570
and or that perhaps we do all have

01:02:21.570 --> 01:02:24.900
different genetic leanings
for different motor functions.

01:02:24.900 --> 01:02:27.600
- Yeah, well, for me, there could be,

01:02:27.600 --> 01:02:29.340
both explanations could be possible.

01:02:29.340 --> 01:02:32.280
For the first, yeah,
I grew up in a family,

01:02:32.280 --> 01:02:34.290
listening to Motown songs,

01:02:34.290 --> 01:02:37.800
you know, dancing, you know,
at parties and so forth,

01:02:37.800 --> 01:02:39.360
family parties and, you know,

01:02:39.360 --> 01:02:41.550
an African American family, basically.

01:02:41.550 --> 01:02:46.550
And so I grew up dancing
from a young child,

01:02:48.420 --> 01:02:53.283
but this discovery, you know,
maybe dancing even moreso,

01:02:54.570 --> 01:02:57.030
in terms of a talent,

01:02:57.030 --> 01:02:58.710
it could, the genetic component,

01:02:58.710 --> 01:03:00.210
if it really exists, I don't know.

01:03:00.210 --> 01:03:03.750
You know, with my 23andMe
results, you know,

01:03:03.750 --> 01:03:07.830
it says I have the genetic substitutions

01:03:07.830 --> 01:03:10.200
that are associated with, you know,

01:03:10.200 --> 01:03:13.170
high intensity athletes
and fast twitch muscles.

01:03:13.170 --> 01:03:14.160
And who knows.

01:03:14.160 --> 01:03:16.140
Maybe that could have something to do with

01:03:16.140 --> 01:03:19.810
me being able to synchronize my body

01:03:20.700 --> 01:03:23.910
to rhythmic sounds, maybe,

01:03:23.910 --> 01:03:25.460
maybe, better than some others.

01:03:26.430 --> 01:03:28.920
It turns out that my genetics also show

01:03:28.920 --> 01:03:31.350
that I have a genetic substitute

01:03:31.350 --> 01:03:34.680
that makes it hard for
me to sing on pitch.

01:03:34.680 --> 01:03:37.080
And so that does correlate
with my, you know,

01:03:37.080 --> 01:03:38.280
even though I can sing on this pitch,

01:03:38.280 --> 01:03:40.890
especially if I hear a piano or, you know,

01:03:40.890 --> 01:03:42.510
kind of playing it, but,

01:03:42.510 --> 01:03:44.640
you know, maybe that's
why my siblings, you know,

01:03:44.640 --> 01:03:47.010
who didn't have that
genetic predisposition

01:03:47.010 --> 01:03:49.440
in his 23andMe results, you know,

01:03:49.440 --> 01:03:52.620
it could go along with the
genetic component, as well.

01:03:52.620 --> 01:03:56.160
- I'm imagining family
gatherings with 23andMe data

01:03:56.160 --> 01:03:58.290
and intense arguments about it,

01:03:58.290 --> 01:03:59.940
innate and learned ability.
- Yes.

01:03:59.940 --> 01:04:01.170
- Fun.

01:04:01.170 --> 01:04:02.850
Love to be an attendant.

01:04:02.850 --> 01:04:04.890
I'm not inviting myself to
your Thanksgiving dinner

01:04:04.890 --> 01:04:07.170
by the way, but I suppose I am.

01:04:07.170 --> 01:04:09.270
- You're welcome to.
- Thank you.

01:04:09.270 --> 01:04:11.520
I'll bring my 23andMe data.

01:04:11.520 --> 01:04:14.670
I'd love to chat a moment
about facial expression

01:04:14.670 --> 01:04:17.213
because that's a form
of motor pattern that,

01:04:17.213 --> 01:04:19.363
you know, I think for
most people out there

01:04:20.370 --> 01:04:22.350
just think about smiling and frowning,

01:04:22.350 --> 01:04:24.480
but there are, of course,
you know, thousands,

01:04:24.480 --> 01:04:27.450
if not millions of micro expressions

01:04:27.450 --> 01:04:28.650
and things of that sort,

01:04:28.650 --> 01:04:30.990
many of which are subconscious.

01:04:30.990 --> 01:04:34.680
And we are all familiar with the fact that

01:04:34.680 --> 01:04:36.270
when what somebody says

01:04:36.270 --> 01:04:38.400
doesn't match some specific feature

01:04:38.400 --> 01:04:40.350
of their facial expression

01:04:40.350 --> 01:04:42.510
that it can call, you know,

01:04:42.510 --> 01:04:45.210
that mismatch can cue our attention,

01:04:45.210 --> 01:04:47.910
especially among people that
know each other very well.

01:04:47.910 --> 01:04:50.430
Like somebody will say,
well, you said that,

01:04:50.430 --> 01:04:53.580
but your right eye
twitched to the, you know,

01:04:53.580 --> 01:04:55.350
a little bit in a way that tells me

01:04:55.350 --> 01:04:56.490
that you didn't really mean that,

01:04:56.490 --> 01:04:57.690
these kinds of things.

01:04:57.690 --> 01:05:01.800
Or when, in the opposite example,

01:05:01.800 --> 01:05:05.040
when the emotionality and
the content of our speech

01:05:05.040 --> 01:05:07.200
is matched to a facial expression,

01:05:07.200 --> 01:05:11.250
there's something that's
just so wonderful about that,

01:05:11.250 --> 01:05:12.990
because it seems like
everything's aligned.

01:05:12.990 --> 01:05:13.823
- [Erich] Yeah.

01:05:13.823 --> 01:05:16.290
- So how does the motor circuitry

01:05:16.290 --> 01:05:18.000
that controls facial expression

01:05:18.000 --> 01:05:21.600
map onto the brain circuits
that control language,

01:05:21.600 --> 01:05:23.400
speech, and even bodily
and hand movements?

01:05:23.400 --> 01:05:26.100
- Yeah, and you ask a great question

01:05:26.100 --> 01:05:28.020
because we both know some colleagues

01:05:28.020 --> 01:05:30.630
like Winrich Freiwald at
Rockefeller University

01:05:30.630 --> 01:05:33.990
who study facial expression
and the neurology behind it.

01:05:33.990 --> 01:05:37.530
And now we both share some
students that we're co-mentoring.

01:05:37.530 --> 01:05:41.310
And talk about this same
question that you brought up.

01:05:41.310 --> 01:05:43.170
And what I'm learning a lot

01:05:43.170 --> 01:05:46.590
is that non-human primates
have a lot of diversity

01:05:46.590 --> 01:05:49.200
in their facial expression
like we humans do.

01:05:49.200 --> 01:05:52.140
And what we know about the neurobiology

01:05:52.140 --> 01:05:55.620
of brain regions controlling
those muscles of the face

01:05:55.620 --> 01:05:57.150
is that these non-human primates

01:05:57.150 --> 01:05:58.830
and some other species that don't learn

01:05:58.830 --> 01:06:00.750
how to imitate vocalizations,

01:06:00.750 --> 01:06:05.750
they have strong connections
from the cortical regions

01:06:05.790 --> 01:06:09.360
to the motor neurons that
control facial expressions,

01:06:09.360 --> 01:06:12.420
but absent connections or weak connections

01:06:12.420 --> 01:06:14.760
to the motor neurons
that control the voice.

01:06:14.760 --> 01:06:18.930
So I think our diverse facial expression,

01:06:18.930 --> 01:06:21.030
even though it's more diverse
in these non-human primates,

01:06:21.030 --> 01:06:25.680
there was already a preexisting
diversity of communication,

01:06:25.680 --> 01:06:28.080
whether it's intentional or unconscious

01:06:28.080 --> 01:06:31.500
through facial expression
in our ancestors.

01:06:31.500 --> 01:06:35.760
And on top of that, we
humans now add the voice

01:06:35.760 --> 01:06:38.100
along with those facial expressions.

01:06:38.100 --> 01:06:38.933
- I see.

01:06:39.886 --> 01:06:42.780
And in terms of language
learning when we're kids,

01:06:42.780 --> 01:06:45.210
I mean, children, fortunately are not told

01:06:45.210 --> 01:06:46.380
to fake their expressions

01:06:46.380 --> 01:06:48.483
or to smile when they say I'm happy.

01:06:49.380 --> 01:06:52.950
So at some point, everybody
learns, for better or for worse,

01:06:52.950 --> 01:06:57.950
how to untangle these different
components of hand movement,

01:06:58.530 --> 01:07:01.410
body posture, speech,
and facial expression.

01:07:01.410 --> 01:07:02.243
- [Erich] Yes.

01:07:02.243 --> 01:07:05.700
- But in their best form, I would say,

01:07:05.700 --> 01:07:07.470
assuming that the best form is always,

01:07:07.470 --> 01:07:09.030
I guess there are
instances where, you know,

01:07:09.030 --> 01:07:10.650
for safety reasons, one might need

01:07:10.650 --> 01:07:13.920
to fain some of these aspects of language.

01:07:13.920 --> 01:07:16.740
But in most cases, when those are aligned,

01:07:16.740 --> 01:07:19.650
it seems like that could reflect that

01:07:19.650 --> 01:07:22.320
all the different circuitries
are operating in parallel,

01:07:22.320 --> 01:07:25.470
but that the ability to misalign these

01:07:25.470 --> 01:07:29.910
is also a powerful
aspect to our maturation.

01:07:29.910 --> 01:07:31.830
I can think of theater, for instance,

01:07:31.830 --> 01:07:36.720
where deliberate disentangling
of these areas is important.

01:07:36.720 --> 01:07:39.390
But also we know when an actor,

01:07:39.390 --> 01:07:41.340
when it feels real.
- Yep.

01:07:41.340 --> 01:07:42.690
- And when it looks like,

01:07:42.690 --> 01:07:44.520
when bad acting is oftentimes

01:07:44.520 --> 01:07:46.500
when the facial expression or body posture

01:07:46.500 --> 01:07:48.060
just doesn't quite match
what we're hearing.

01:07:48.060 --> 01:07:48.930
- [Erich] Yeah.

01:07:48.930 --> 01:07:52.170
- So are these skills that people,

01:07:52.170 --> 01:07:53.610
that learn and acquire according

01:07:53.610 --> 01:07:55.230
to adaptability and profession?

01:07:55.230 --> 01:07:58.590
Or do you think that all
children and all adults

01:07:58.590 --> 01:08:00.000
eventually learn how to couple

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:02.460
and uncouple these circuits a little bit?

01:08:02.460 --> 01:08:05.250
- Yeah, I think it's this similar argument

01:08:05.250 --> 01:08:08.370
I mentioned earlier about
the innate and learned

01:08:08.370 --> 01:08:09.600
for the vocalizations.

01:08:09.600 --> 01:08:10.950
And by the way, when I say,

01:08:10.950 --> 01:08:12.720
we humans have facial expressions

01:08:12.720 --> 01:08:14.640
associated with our vocalizations

01:08:14.640 --> 01:08:17.040
in a different way than
primates, non-human primates,

01:08:17.040 --> 01:08:19.590
it's the learned vocalizations
I'm talking about.

01:08:19.590 --> 01:08:23.700
So there is a common view out there

01:08:23.700 --> 01:08:26.430
that facial expressions
in non-human species

01:08:26.430 --> 01:08:28.140
like nonhuman primates,

01:08:28.140 --> 01:08:30.240
or you can have them in birds, too,

01:08:30.240 --> 01:08:33.480
are innate, all right.

01:08:33.480 --> 01:08:36.240
And so they're reflexive and controlled.

01:08:36.240 --> 01:08:37.073
I don't believe that.

01:08:37.073 --> 01:08:38.850
I think there's some
learned component to it.

01:08:38.850 --> 01:08:41.880
And I think we have more
learning component to it as well,

01:08:41.880 --> 01:08:44.880
but we also have an innate component.

01:08:44.880 --> 01:08:48.300
And so if you try to put
your hands behind your back

01:08:48.300 --> 01:08:49.890
and hold your fist, or even just not,

01:08:49.890 --> 01:08:52.440
and try to speak and try to communicate,

01:08:52.440 --> 01:08:53.790
it's actually harder to do.

01:08:53.790 --> 01:08:57.150
You have to force yourself
or put it by your side.

01:08:57.150 --> 01:08:58.410
This comes naturally.

01:08:58.410 --> 01:08:59.880
Facial expressions comes naturally

01:08:59.880 --> 01:09:02.070
because there's an innate component.

01:09:02.070 --> 01:09:06.360
And yes, you have to learn
how to dissociate the two,

01:09:06.360 --> 01:09:08.370
communicate something
angry with your hands

01:09:08.370 --> 01:09:09.480
or with your face,

01:09:09.480 --> 01:09:13.590
but, you know, politely with your voice.

01:09:13.590 --> 01:09:16.320
It's very hard to separate those two,

01:09:16.320 --> 01:09:18.330
because there is that innate component

01:09:18.330 --> 01:09:19.773
that brings them together.

01:09:21.180 --> 01:09:22.410
So it's like an email, too.

01:09:22.410 --> 01:09:25.680
You're emailing and someone
says something by email,

01:09:25.680 --> 01:09:29.463
someone can interpret
that angrily or gently,

01:09:30.420 --> 01:09:32.670
and it becomes ambiguous.

01:09:32.670 --> 01:09:35.283
The facial expressions
get rid of that ambiguity.

01:09:36.150 --> 01:09:37.020
- So glad you brought that up

01:09:37.020 --> 01:09:38.670
because my next question was,

01:09:38.670 --> 01:09:41.310
and is about written language.

01:09:41.310 --> 01:09:44.100
The first question I'll
ask is when you write,

01:09:44.100 --> 01:09:46.770
either type or write things out by hand,

01:09:46.770 --> 01:09:51.770
do you hear the content
of what you want to write

01:09:51.870 --> 01:09:52.830
in your head?

01:09:52.830 --> 01:09:54.600
Just, you personally.

01:09:54.600 --> 01:09:55.740
- Yes, I do.

01:09:55.740 --> 01:09:59.580
Yeah, and I know that I do,

01:09:59.580 --> 01:10:03.540
because I was trying to figure
out a debate about this issue

01:10:03.540 --> 01:10:06.030
and trying to resolve the debate

01:10:06.030 --> 01:10:09.060
with my own self experimentation on me.

01:10:09.060 --> 01:10:11.531
- I asked that because,

01:10:11.531 --> 01:10:12.720
a quite well-known colleague of ours,

01:10:12.720 --> 01:10:14.700
Karl Deisseroth at Stanford,

01:10:14.700 --> 01:10:16.879
who's been on this podcast, you know,

01:10:16.879 --> 01:10:18.810
his optogenetics fame and
psychiatry fame, et cetera.

01:10:18.810 --> 01:10:21.757
- Yeah, I know him.
- Yeah, he sends his regards.

01:10:21.757 --> 01:10:22.860
- Okay. [laughs]

01:10:22.860 --> 01:10:27.300
- Told me that his practice for writing

01:10:27.300 --> 01:10:32.300
and for thinking involves
a quite painful process

01:10:32.310 --> 01:10:36.180
of forcing himself to sit completely still

01:10:36.180 --> 01:10:38.370
and think in complete sentences,

01:10:38.370 --> 01:10:40.047
to force thinking in complete sentences.

01:10:40.047 --> 01:10:41.310
And when he told me that,

01:10:41.310 --> 01:10:43.950
I decided to try this exercise
and it's quite difficult.

01:10:43.950 --> 01:10:44.880
First of all, it's difficult

01:10:44.880 --> 01:10:46.590
for the reason that you mentioned,

01:10:46.590 --> 01:10:48.360
which is that with many thoughts,

01:10:48.360 --> 01:10:49.193
I want to look around

01:10:49.193 --> 01:10:52.140
and I start to gesticulate
with my hands, right?

01:10:52.140 --> 01:10:53.010
So there it is, again,

01:10:53.010 --> 01:10:55.680
the connection between
language and hand movement,

01:10:55.680 --> 01:10:57.123
even if one isn't speaking.

01:10:58.470 --> 01:11:01.860
And the other part that's challenging is

01:11:01.860 --> 01:11:05.160
I realize that while we
write in complete sentences,

01:11:05.160 --> 01:11:08.057
most of the time, we'll talk
about how that's changing now.

01:11:08.057 --> 01:11:10.140
- Right.
- In texting, et cetera.

01:11:10.140 --> 01:11:14.610
That we don't often think
in complete sentences,

01:11:14.610 --> 01:11:18.750
and specifically in simple
declarative sentences,

01:11:18.750 --> 01:11:20.730
that a lot of our thoughts would be,

01:11:20.730 --> 01:11:23.340
if they were written out onto a page

01:11:23.340 --> 01:11:27.300
would look pretty much
like passive language

01:11:27.300 --> 01:11:30.360
that a good copy editor or
a good editor would say,

01:11:30.360 --> 01:11:31.950
ugh, like we need to cross this out,

01:11:31.950 --> 01:11:33.630
make this simple and declarative.

01:11:33.630 --> 01:11:35.880
So what I'm getting at here is

01:11:35.880 --> 01:11:40.170
what is the process of going
from a thought to language,

01:11:40.170 --> 01:11:42.180
to written word?

01:11:42.180 --> 01:11:46.920
And I also wanted to touch
on handwritten versus typed,

01:11:46.920 --> 01:11:49.650
but thought to language, to written word.

01:11:49.650 --> 01:11:50.700
What's going on there?

01:11:50.700 --> 01:11:53.070
What do we know about
the neural circuitry?

01:11:53.070 --> 01:11:55.470
And I was going to ask, why is it so hard?

01:11:55.470 --> 01:11:58.740
But now I want to ask why
is this even possible?

01:11:58.740 --> 01:12:00.600
It seems like a very challenging

01:12:00.600 --> 01:12:02.250
neural computational problem.

01:12:02.250 --> 01:12:03.690
- Yeah, yeah.

01:12:03.690 --> 01:12:07.860
And coming from the linguistic world,

01:12:07.860 --> 01:12:10.080
and even just the regular
neurobiology world,

01:12:10.080 --> 01:12:11.790
going back to something I said before

01:12:11.790 --> 01:12:14.400
about a separate language
module in the brain.

01:12:14.400 --> 01:12:16.740
You know, there was this
thought or hypothesis

01:12:16.740 --> 01:12:18.570
that this language module

01:12:18.570 --> 01:12:21.690
has all these complex algorithms to them.

01:12:21.690 --> 01:12:24.480
And they're signaling
to the speech circuit,

01:12:24.480 --> 01:12:26.430
how to produce the sounds,

01:12:26.430 --> 01:12:29.253
the hand circuit, how to
write them or gesture,

01:12:30.240 --> 01:12:34.380
the visual pathway on how to
interpret them from reading

01:12:34.380 --> 01:12:36.330
and the auditory pathway for listening.

01:12:37.290 --> 01:12:39.930
I don't think that's the case, all right.

01:12:39.930 --> 01:12:41.700
And you know, that this thinking where

01:12:41.700 --> 01:12:43.890
there's this internal speech going on.

01:12:43.890 --> 01:12:46.050
What I think is going on is

01:12:46.050 --> 01:12:48.990
to explain what you're asking is about,

01:12:48.990 --> 01:12:50.490
that I'm going to take
it from the perspective,

01:12:50.490 --> 01:12:51.660
reading something.

01:12:51.660 --> 01:12:53.790
You read something on a paper.

01:12:53.790 --> 01:12:56.760
The signal from the paper
goes through your eyes.

01:12:56.760 --> 01:12:58.080
It goes to the back of your brain,

01:12:58.080 --> 01:13:01.440
to your visual cortical
regions eventually.

01:13:01.440 --> 01:13:04.080
And then you now got to
interpret that signal

01:13:04.080 --> 01:13:07.110
in your visual pathway
of what you're reading.

01:13:07.110 --> 01:13:09.270
How are you going to do
that in terms of speech?

01:13:09.270 --> 01:13:12.620
That visual signal then
goes to your speech pathway

01:13:12.620 --> 01:13:15.780
in the motor cortex in
front here, in Broca's area.

01:13:15.780 --> 01:13:18.780
And you silently speak what you read

01:13:18.780 --> 01:13:21.330
in your brain without moving your muscles.

01:13:21.330 --> 01:13:24.483
And sometimes actually, if
you put electrodes, EEG,

01:13:25.800 --> 01:13:28.950
EMG electrodes on your laryngeal muscles,

01:13:28.950 --> 01:13:30.570
even on birds, you can do this,

01:13:30.570 --> 01:13:33.870
you'll see activity there while reading

01:13:33.870 --> 01:13:36.390
or trying to speak silently,

01:13:36.390 --> 01:13:38.550
even though no sound's coming out.

01:13:38.550 --> 01:13:42.030
And so your speech pathway

01:13:42.030 --> 01:13:44.703
is now speaking what you're reading.

01:13:45.540 --> 01:13:47.550
Now to finish it off,

01:13:47.550 --> 01:13:49.770
that signal is sent to
your auditory pathways

01:13:49.770 --> 01:13:53.280
so you can hear what you're
speaking in your own head.

01:13:53.280 --> 01:13:54.120
- That's incredible.

01:13:54.120 --> 01:13:56.130
- And this is why it's complicated

01:13:56.130 --> 01:13:59.160
because you're using like
three different pathways,

01:13:59.160 --> 01:14:01.137
the visual, the speaking motor one,

01:14:01.137 --> 01:14:04.170
and the auditory to read.

01:14:04.170 --> 01:14:06.900
Oh, and then you got to write, right?

01:14:06.900 --> 01:14:08.700
Okay, here comes the fourth one.

01:14:08.700 --> 01:14:11.490
Now the hand areas next
to your speech pathway

01:14:11.490 --> 01:14:13.290
has got to take that auditory signal

01:14:13.290 --> 01:14:16.110
or even the adjacent
motor signals for speaking

01:14:16.110 --> 01:14:19.290
and translate it into a
visual signal on paper.

01:14:19.290 --> 01:14:22.293
So, you're using at least
four brain circuits,

01:14:23.430 --> 01:14:25.470
which includes the speech production

01:14:25.470 --> 01:14:28.350
and the speech perception
pathways to write.

01:14:28.350 --> 01:14:29.550
- Incredible.

01:14:29.550 --> 01:14:33.030
And finally, explain to me why,

01:14:33.030 --> 01:14:35.700
so I was weaned teaching undergraduates,

01:14:35.700 --> 01:14:37.230
graduate students and medical students

01:14:37.230 --> 01:14:40.560
and I've observed that when I'm teaching,

01:14:40.560 --> 01:14:41.970
I have to stop speaking

01:14:41.970 --> 01:14:44.250
if I'm going to write
something on the board.

01:14:44.250 --> 01:14:46.680
I just have to stop all
speaking completely.

01:14:46.680 --> 01:14:47.719
- [Erich] Right.

01:14:47.719 --> 01:14:49.140
- It turns out this is
an advantage to catch

01:14:49.140 --> 01:14:51.360
because it allows me to catch my voice.

01:14:51.360 --> 01:14:53.910
It allows me to slow down a bit, you know,

01:14:53.910 --> 01:14:56.460
breathe and inhale some oxygen and so on

01:14:56.460 --> 01:14:57.690
because I tend to speak quickly

01:14:57.690 --> 01:14:59.850
if I'm not writing something out.

01:14:59.850 --> 01:15:02.040
So there's a break in
the circuitry for me,

01:15:02.040 --> 01:15:03.630
or at least they are distinct enough

01:15:03.630 --> 01:15:06.570
that I have to stop and
then write something out.

01:15:06.570 --> 01:15:10.380
- Yes, that does imply
competing brain circuits

01:15:10.380 --> 01:15:13.890
for your conscious attention.

01:15:13.890 --> 01:15:16.920
- We have colleagues up at Columbia Med

01:15:16.920 --> 01:15:19.980
who are known, at least in our circles,

01:15:19.980 --> 01:15:23.610
for voice dictating their
papers, not writing them out,

01:15:23.610 --> 01:15:26.250
but just speaking into a voice recorder.

01:15:26.250 --> 01:15:27.720
I've written papers that way.

01:15:27.720 --> 01:15:31.470
It doesn't feel quite as natural for me

01:15:31.470 --> 01:15:32.880
as writing things out.
- Yeah.

01:15:32.880 --> 01:15:35.670
- But not because I can
go quickly from thought

01:15:35.670 --> 01:15:36.883
to language to typing.

01:15:36.883 --> 01:15:38.250
I type reasonably fast.

01:15:38.250 --> 01:15:39.330
I can touch type now.

01:15:39.330 --> 01:15:40.590
I don't think I ever taught my,

01:15:40.590 --> 01:15:41.610
I think I taught myself.

01:15:41.610 --> 01:15:43.230
I never took a touch typing course.

01:15:43.230 --> 01:15:44.063
But it just sort of happened.

01:15:44.063 --> 01:15:45.810
Now, I think, my motor system

01:15:45.810 --> 01:15:47.340
seems to know where the keys are

01:15:47.340 --> 01:15:51.333
with enough accuracy, that it works.

01:15:53.070 --> 01:15:56.280
This is remarkable to me
that any of us can do this.

01:15:56.280 --> 01:15:59.310
But when it comes to writing,

01:15:59.310 --> 01:16:02.940
what I've found is that
if my rate of thought

01:16:02.940 --> 01:16:07.053
and my rate of writing are
aligned nicely, things go well.

01:16:07.980 --> 01:16:11.400
However, if I'm thinking
much faster than I can write,

01:16:11.400 --> 01:16:12.720
that's a problem.

01:16:12.720 --> 01:16:15.180
And certainly, if I'm thinking more slowly

01:16:15.180 --> 01:16:17.940
than I want to write,
that's also a problem.

01:16:17.940 --> 01:16:19.590
And the solution for me

01:16:19.590 --> 01:16:21.480
has been to write with a pen.

01:16:21.480 --> 01:16:22.530
I'm in love with these.

01:16:22.530 --> 01:16:24.450
And I have no relationship to the company,

01:16:24.450 --> 01:16:26.130
at least not now, although
if they want to come,

01:16:26.130 --> 01:16:27.840
you know, if they want to work with us,

01:16:27.840 --> 01:16:29.850
I love these Pilot V5, V7's

01:16:29.850 --> 01:16:32.490
because not necessarily because of the ink

01:16:32.490 --> 01:16:34.590
or the feel, although I like that as well.

01:16:34.590 --> 01:16:37.260
But because of the rate
that it allows me to write,

01:16:37.260 --> 01:16:38.580
they write very well slowly,

01:16:38.580 --> 01:16:40.500
and they write very well quickly.

01:16:40.500 --> 01:16:43.113
And so I have this theory,

01:16:43.980 --> 01:16:46.650
supported only by my own anecdata,

01:16:46.650 --> 01:16:48.240
no peer reviewed study,

01:16:48.240 --> 01:16:53.240
that writing by hand is
fundamentally different

01:16:53.280 --> 01:16:55.740
than typing out information.

01:16:55.740 --> 01:17:00.740
Is there any evidence that
this motor pathway for writing

01:17:00.810 --> 01:17:04.380
is better or somehow different

01:17:04.380 --> 01:17:06.930
than the motor pathway for typing?

01:17:06.930 --> 01:17:09.360
- Yeah, that's interesting.

01:17:09.360 --> 01:17:11.700
And I don't know of any studies.

01:17:11.700 --> 01:17:13.470
I have my own personal experience as well,

01:17:13.470 --> 01:17:16.200
but trying to put this into the context,

01:17:16.200 --> 01:17:18.060
if I had to, you know,

01:17:18.060 --> 01:17:21.295
design an experiment to test
the hypothesis here that,

01:17:21.295 --> 01:17:23.670
you know, to explain
your experience and mine,

01:17:23.670 --> 01:17:26.700
is that writing by hand,

01:17:26.700 --> 01:17:31.700
I would argue, requires a
different set of less skills

01:17:32.070 --> 01:17:35.040
with the fingers than typing.

01:17:35.040 --> 01:17:38.250
So you have to coordinate
your fingers more

01:17:38.250 --> 01:17:42.690
in opposite directions
and so forth with typing,

01:17:42.690 --> 01:17:45.423
but also writing by hand
requires more arm movement.

01:17:46.445 --> 01:17:49.120
And so therefore, I would argue that

01:17:52.474 --> 01:17:55.200
the difficulty there could be

01:17:55.200 --> 01:17:58.830
in the types of muscles
and the fine motor control

01:17:58.830 --> 01:18:00.150
you need of those muscles

01:18:00.150 --> 01:18:02.490
along with speaking in your
brain at the same time.

01:18:02.490 --> 01:18:04.530
- So basically, I'm a course, I'm a brute.

01:18:04.530 --> 01:18:06.013
So it makes sense that I would have,

01:18:06.013 --> 01:18:07.980
a more primitive writing
device would work.

01:18:07.980 --> 01:18:09.090
- That's right, yes.

01:18:09.090 --> 01:18:11.320
But, let me answer this in terms

01:18:13.259 --> 01:18:14.733
of my own personal experience, right.

01:18:14.733 --> 01:18:19.733
What I find is I can write
something faster by hand

01:18:22.380 --> 01:18:25.590
for a short period of
time, compared to typing.

01:18:25.590 --> 01:18:26.820
And that is because I think

01:18:26.820 --> 01:18:30.720
I run out of the energy
in my arm movements

01:18:30.720 --> 01:18:32.790
faster than I run out of muscle energy

01:18:32.790 --> 01:18:34.473
in my finger movements.

01:18:35.580 --> 01:18:38.460
And I think it takes longer time

01:18:38.460 --> 01:18:41.310
for us to write words with our fingers,

01:18:41.310 --> 01:18:43.830
because, and in terms of the speech.

01:18:43.830 --> 01:18:46.080
So I think your writing,

01:18:46.080 --> 01:18:49.290
whether it's by hand or
typing and your speech,

01:18:49.290 --> 01:18:51.690
they only will align very well

01:18:51.690 --> 01:18:54.540
if you can type as fast as you can speak

01:18:54.540 --> 01:18:56.610
or write as fast as you
can speak in your head.

01:18:56.610 --> 01:18:57.443
- I love it.

01:18:57.443 --> 01:18:59.400
So what you've done, if
I understand correctly,

01:18:59.400 --> 01:19:02.730
is created a bridge between
thought and writing,

01:19:02.730 --> 01:19:04.740
and that bridge is speech.

01:19:04.740 --> 01:19:07.080
- That bridge is speech, that's right.

01:19:07.080 --> 01:19:07.913
That's right.

01:19:07.913 --> 01:19:09.120
When you're writing something out,

01:19:09.120 --> 01:19:11.190
you're speaking it to yourself.

01:19:11.190 --> 01:19:13.440
And if you're speaking
faster than you can type,

01:19:13.440 --> 01:19:14.490
you've got a problem.

01:19:15.390 --> 01:19:16.874
- Interesting.

01:19:16.874 --> 01:19:19.410
I do a number of podcast episodes
that are not with guests,

01:19:19.410 --> 01:19:20.400
but solo episodes.

01:19:20.400 --> 01:19:21.450
And as listeners know,

01:19:21.450 --> 01:19:24.630
these are very long episodes,
often two or more hours.

01:19:24.630 --> 01:19:27.600
And we joke around the podcast studio

01:19:27.600 --> 01:19:30.540
that I will get locked
into a mode of speech

01:19:30.540 --> 01:19:34.320
where some of it is more
collaborative and anecdotal

01:19:34.320 --> 01:19:38.460
and then I'll punch out
simple declarative sentences.

01:19:38.460 --> 01:19:42.090
I find it very hard to switch
from one module to the next.

01:19:42.090 --> 01:19:43.950
The thing that I have done

01:19:43.950 --> 01:19:47.430
in order to make that
transition more fluid

01:19:47.430 --> 01:19:49.920
and prep for those podcast episodes

01:19:49.920 --> 01:19:53.430
is actually to read the lyrics of songs

01:19:53.430 --> 01:19:55.710
and to sing them in my head

01:19:55.710 --> 01:19:57.720
as a way of warming up my vocal chords.

01:19:57.720 --> 01:20:01.290
But luckily for those
around me, when I do that,

01:20:01.290 --> 01:20:03.750
I'm not actually singing out loud.

01:20:03.750 --> 01:20:06.990
And so this, what you're telling me

01:20:06.990 --> 01:20:11.990
supports this idea that even
when we are imagining singing

01:20:12.150 --> 01:20:14.220
or writing in our mind,

01:20:14.220 --> 01:20:16.470
we are exercising our vocal chords.

01:20:16.470 --> 01:20:19.800
- You're actually getting
little low potentials

01:20:19.800 --> 01:20:22.770
of electrical currents
reaching your muscles there,

01:20:22.770 --> 01:20:24.210
which also means you're exercising

01:20:24.210 --> 01:20:27.060
your speech brain circuits too,
without actually, you know,

01:20:27.060 --> 01:20:29.910
going with the full-blown
activity in the muscles.

01:20:29.910 --> 01:20:30.743
- Incredible.
- Yeah.

01:20:30.743 --> 01:20:33.933
And this idea of singing
helps you as well.

01:20:36.000 --> 01:20:38.070
Even with Parkinson's
patients and so forth,

01:20:38.070 --> 01:20:39.030
when they want to say something,

01:20:39.030 --> 01:20:41.310
singing or listening to
music helps them move better.

01:20:41.310 --> 01:20:45.180
And the idea there is that the
brain circuits for singing,

01:20:45.180 --> 01:20:47.940
or let's say the function of
the brain circuits for speech

01:20:47.940 --> 01:20:51.450
being used for singing first
is the more ancestral trait.

01:20:51.450 --> 01:20:54.270
And that's why it's easier
to do things with singing

01:20:54.270 --> 01:20:56.220
sometimes than it is with speaking.

01:20:56.220 --> 01:20:57.053
- I love it.

01:20:58.170 --> 01:21:02.280
Stutter is a particularly interesting case

01:21:02.280 --> 01:21:04.860
and one that every once in a while,

01:21:04.860 --> 01:21:07.260
I'll get questions about
this from our audience.

01:21:08.340 --> 01:21:11.100
Stutter is complicated
in a number of ways,

01:21:11.100 --> 01:21:14.550
but culturally, and my
understanding from these emails

01:21:14.550 --> 01:21:15.840
that I receive is that

01:21:15.840 --> 01:21:20.190
stutter can often cause
people to hide and speak less

01:21:20.190 --> 01:21:21.990
because it can be embarrassing.

01:21:21.990 --> 01:21:25.170
And we are often not patient with stutter.

01:21:25.170 --> 01:21:27.540
We also have the assumption
that if somebody's stuttering,

01:21:27.540 --> 01:21:28.620
that they're thinking is slow,

01:21:28.620 --> 01:21:30.930
but it turns out there are many examples,

01:21:30.930 --> 01:21:33.150
historically of people
who could not speak well,

01:21:33.150 --> 01:21:35.433
but who were brilliant thinkers.

01:21:36.390 --> 01:21:37.920
I don't know how well they could write,

01:21:37.920 --> 01:21:40.413
but they found other
modes of communication.

01:21:41.340 --> 01:21:45.120
I realize that you're not a
speech pathologist or therapist,

01:21:45.120 --> 01:21:48.240
but what is the current neurobiological

01:21:48.240 --> 01:21:49.440
understanding of stutter

01:21:49.440 --> 01:21:51.900
and, or what's being developed

01:21:51.900 --> 01:21:53.550
in terms of treatments for stutter?

01:21:53.550 --> 01:21:57.780
- Yeah, so we actually accidentally

01:21:57.780 --> 01:22:00.300
came across stuttering in songbirds.

01:22:00.300 --> 01:22:03.000
And we've published several papers on this

01:22:03.000 --> 01:22:04.920
to try to figure out the
neurobiological basis.

01:22:04.920 --> 01:22:08.400
The first study we had was a brain area

01:22:08.400 --> 01:22:09.600
called the basal ganglio,

01:22:09.600 --> 01:22:12.990
or the striatum part of the basal ganglia

01:22:12.990 --> 01:22:14.910
involved in coordinating movements,

01:22:14.910 --> 01:22:16.830
learning how to make movements,

01:22:16.830 --> 01:22:21.830
when it was damaged in a
speech-like pathway in these birds,

01:22:22.110 --> 01:22:25.260
what we found is that
they started to stutter

01:22:25.260 --> 01:22:27.183
as the brain region recovered.

01:22:28.080 --> 01:22:30.750
And unlike humans,

01:22:30.750 --> 01:22:33.750
they actually recovered
after three or four months.

01:22:33.750 --> 01:22:34.860
And why is that the case?

01:22:34.860 --> 01:22:38.130
Because bird brains
undergoes new neurogenesis

01:22:38.130 --> 01:22:41.790
in a way that human or
mammal brains don't.

01:22:41.790 --> 01:22:45.000
And it was the new neurons
that were coming in

01:22:45.000 --> 01:22:47.850
into the circuit, but not quite, you know,

01:22:47.850 --> 01:22:50.580
with the right proper activity

01:22:50.580 --> 01:22:54.480
was resulting in this
stuttering, in these birds.

01:22:54.480 --> 01:22:55.890
And after it was repaired,

01:22:55.890 --> 01:22:59.820
not exactly the old song
came back after the repair,

01:22:59.820 --> 01:23:02.370
but still it recovered a lot better.

01:23:02.370 --> 01:23:04.020
And it's now known,

01:23:04.020 --> 01:23:08.223
they call this neurogenic
stuttering in humans,

01:23:09.060 --> 01:23:10.500
damage to the basal ganglia

01:23:10.500 --> 01:23:12.450
or some type of disruption

01:23:12.450 --> 01:23:14.430
to the basal ganglia at a young age,

01:23:14.430 --> 01:23:16.710
also causes stuttering in humans.

01:23:16.710 --> 01:23:19.323
And even those who are
born with stuttering,

01:23:21.510 --> 01:23:24.900
it's often the basal
ganglia that's disrupted

01:23:24.900 --> 01:23:26.490
than some other brain circuit

01:23:26.490 --> 01:23:29.460
and we think the speech
part of the basal ganglia.

01:23:29.460 --> 01:23:33.000
- Can adults who maintain
a stutter from childhood

01:23:33.000 --> 01:23:34.410
repair that stutter?

01:23:34.410 --> 01:23:36.810
- They can repair it with therapy,

01:23:36.810 --> 01:23:39.450
with learning how to speak slower,

01:23:39.450 --> 01:23:42.000
learning how to tap out a rhythm.

01:23:42.000 --> 01:23:43.770
And yeah, I'm not a speech pathologist,

01:23:43.770 --> 01:23:45.840
but I started reading this literature

01:23:45.840 --> 01:23:48.000
and talking to others, that you know,

01:23:48.000 --> 01:23:50.490
colleagues who actually study stuttering.

01:23:50.490 --> 01:23:54.930
So yes, there are ways to
overcome the stuttering

01:23:54.930 --> 01:23:58.923
through, you know, behavioral therapy.

01:23:59.760 --> 01:24:04.140
And I think all of the tools out there

01:24:04.140 --> 01:24:07.770
have something to do with
sensory motor integration,

01:24:07.770 --> 01:24:11.310
controlling what you
hear with what you output

01:24:11.310 --> 01:24:15.930
in a thoughtful controlled way
helps reduce the stuttering.

01:24:15.930 --> 01:24:17.670
- There are a couple
examples from real life

01:24:17.670 --> 01:24:18.927
that I want to touch on,

01:24:18.927 --> 01:24:21.319
and one is somewhat facetious,

01:24:21.319 --> 01:24:26.220
but now I realize, is a
serious neurobiological issue,

01:24:26.220 --> 01:24:28.620
serious meaning I think interesting.

01:24:28.620 --> 01:24:31.170
Which is that every once in a while,

01:24:31.170 --> 01:24:33.150
I will have a conversation with somebody

01:24:33.150 --> 01:24:37.050
who says the last word of
the sentence along with me.

01:24:37.050 --> 01:24:39.873
And it seems annoying in some instances,

01:24:40.830 --> 01:24:42.900
but I'm guessing this
is just a breakthrough

01:24:42.900 --> 01:24:43.860
of the motor pattern

01:24:43.860 --> 01:24:46.320
that they're hearing what
I'm saying very well.

01:24:46.320 --> 01:24:47.820
So I'm going to interpret this kindly

01:24:47.820 --> 01:24:50.010
and think they're hearing what I'm saying.

01:24:50.010 --> 01:24:53.550
They're literally hearing it in their mind

01:24:53.550 --> 01:24:56.550
and they're getting that
low-level electrical activity

01:24:56.550 --> 01:24:57.390
to their throat.

01:24:57.390 --> 01:24:58.740
And they're just joining me

01:25:00.209 --> 01:25:02.670
in the enunciation of what I'm saying,

01:25:02.670 --> 01:25:04.680
probably without realizing it.

01:25:04.680 --> 01:25:06.270
Can we assume that that might be the case?

01:25:06.270 --> 01:25:08.790
- Well, I wouldn't be
surprised so that, you know,

01:25:08.790 --> 01:25:10.800
the motor theory of speech perception

01:25:10.800 --> 01:25:12.120
where this idea originally came,

01:25:12.120 --> 01:25:15.420
what you hear is going
through your speech circuit

01:25:15.420 --> 01:25:18.273
and then also activating
those muscles slightly.

01:25:19.440 --> 01:25:23.610
So yes, so one might argue,

01:25:23.610 --> 01:25:26.370
okay, is that speech
circuit now interpreting

01:25:26.370 --> 01:25:27.900
what that person is speaking?

01:25:27.900 --> 01:25:29.520
Now, you're listening to me

01:25:29.520 --> 01:25:30.960
and is going to finish it off

01:25:30.960 --> 01:25:33.870
because it's already
going through their brain

01:25:33.870 --> 01:25:34.800
and they can predict it?

01:25:34.800 --> 01:25:36.900
That would be one theory.

01:25:36.900 --> 01:25:38.820
And I don't think the
verdict out there is known,

01:25:38.820 --> 01:25:39.990
but that's one.

01:25:39.990 --> 01:25:44.990
The other is synchronizing
turn-taking in the conversation

01:25:47.490 --> 01:25:52.050
where you're acknowledging
that we understand each other

01:25:52.050 --> 01:25:54.153
by finishing off what I say.

01:25:55.455 --> 01:25:58.560
And it's almost like a
social bonding kind of thing.

01:25:58.560 --> 01:25:59.580
The other could be,

01:25:59.580 --> 01:26:00.780
I want the person to shut up

01:26:00.780 --> 01:26:03.210
so I can speak as well and take that turn.

01:26:03.210 --> 01:26:08.210
And each pair of people have a
rhythm to their conversation.

01:26:08.310 --> 01:26:10.410
And if you have somebody
who's over talkative

01:26:10.410 --> 01:26:12.840
versus under talkative of vice versa,

01:26:12.840 --> 01:26:15.480
that rhythm can be lost
in them finishing ideas

01:26:15.480 --> 01:26:16.920
and going back and forth.

01:26:16.920 --> 01:26:20.370
But I think having something
to do with turn-taking,

01:26:20.370 --> 01:26:22.320
as well, makes a lot of sense.

01:26:22.320 --> 01:26:24.270
- I have a colleague at Stanford who says

01:26:24.270 --> 01:26:27.379
that interruption is a sign of interest.

01:26:27.379 --> 01:26:28.212
[Erich laughs]

01:26:28.212 --> 01:26:29.190
I'm not sure that everyone agrees.

01:26:29.190 --> 01:26:30.690
I think it's highly contextual.

01:26:30.690 --> 01:26:31.523
- [Erich] Yes.

01:26:31.523 --> 01:26:34.650
- But there is this form of a verbal nod

01:26:34.650 --> 01:26:36.420
of saying, hmm hmm or things of that sort.

01:26:36.420 --> 01:26:38.670
And they're many of these.

01:26:38.670 --> 01:26:40.740
And I'm often told by
my audience, you know,

01:26:40.740 --> 01:26:43.110
that I interrupt my guests
and things of that sort.

01:26:43.110 --> 01:26:45.060
Oftentimes, I'll just get
caught in the natural flow

01:26:45.060 --> 01:26:46.660
of the conversation, but.
- Right.

01:26:46.660 --> 01:26:49.680
Well, I think we've had
pretty good turn-taking here,

01:26:49.680 --> 01:26:50.513
I hope.

01:26:50.513 --> 01:26:51.870
- So far so good.
- I feel that way.

01:26:51.870 --> 01:26:53.070
- I'm glad you feel that way,

01:26:53.070 --> 01:26:54.930
because especially in the
context of a discussion

01:26:54.930 --> 01:26:56.250
about language.
- Yes.

01:26:56.250 --> 01:26:57.303
- It seems important.

01:26:59.190 --> 01:27:04.190
Texting is a very, very
interesting evolution of language

01:27:05.670 --> 01:27:09.540
because what you've told us
is that we have a thought,

01:27:09.540 --> 01:27:11.520
it's translated into language.

01:27:11.520 --> 01:27:13.020
It might not be complete sentences,

01:27:13.020 --> 01:27:15.750
but texting, I have to
imagine this is the first time

01:27:15.750 --> 01:27:18.600
in human evolution where
we've written with our thumbs.

01:27:18.600 --> 01:27:20.010
So I don't know,

01:27:20.010 --> 01:27:22.050
it seems more primitive to
me than typing with fingers

01:27:22.050 --> 01:27:23.070
or writing with hands, but hey,

01:27:23.070 --> 01:27:25.740
who am I to judge the
evolution of our species

01:27:25.740 --> 01:27:27.300
in one direction or the other?

01:27:27.300 --> 01:27:31.380
But the shorthand grammatically,

01:27:31.380 --> 01:27:34.650
often grammatically deficient
incomplete sentence form

01:27:34.650 --> 01:27:37.833
of texting is an incredible thing to see.

01:27:38.760 --> 01:27:41.310
Early in relationships,
romantic relationships,

01:27:41.310 --> 01:27:44.280
people will often evaluate the others text

01:27:44.280 --> 01:27:47.100
and their ability to use proper grammar

01:27:47.100 --> 01:27:48.510
and spelling, et cetera.

01:27:48.510 --> 01:27:50.670
This often quickly degrades.

01:27:50.670 --> 01:27:51.870
And there's an acceptance

01:27:51.870 --> 01:27:54.330
that we're just trying to
communicate through shorthand,

01:27:54.330 --> 01:27:57.900
almost military like shorthand,

01:27:57.900 --> 01:28:00.690
but with internally
consistent between people,

01:28:00.690 --> 01:28:02.940
but there's no general
consensus of what things mean,

01:28:02.940 --> 01:28:05.940
but, you know, WTFs and like,

01:28:05.940 --> 01:28:07.590
and OMGs and all sorts of things.

01:28:07.590 --> 01:28:08.423
- [Erich] Right.

01:28:09.390 --> 01:28:11.370
- I wonder sometimes whether or not

01:28:11.370 --> 01:28:14.820
we are getting less proficient at speech

01:28:14.820 --> 01:28:19.170
because we are not
required to write and think

01:28:19.170 --> 01:28:21.690
in complete sentences.
- Hmm hmm.

01:28:21.690 --> 01:28:22.920
- I'm not being judgemental here.

01:28:22.920 --> 01:28:24.360
I see this in my colleagues.

01:28:24.360 --> 01:28:25.980
I see this in myself.

01:28:25.980 --> 01:28:28.983
This is not a judgment of
the younger generation.

01:28:29.910 --> 01:28:34.890
I also know that slang
has existed for decades,

01:28:34.890 --> 01:28:36.360
if not hundreds of years.

01:28:36.360 --> 01:28:38.280
But I also know that I
don't speak the same way

01:28:38.280 --> 01:28:40.260
that I did when I was a teenager,

01:28:40.260 --> 01:28:42.330
because I've suppressed
a lot of that slang,

01:28:42.330 --> 01:28:45.300
not because it's
inappropriate or offensive,

01:28:45.300 --> 01:28:46.953
although some of it was, frankly,

01:28:48.120 --> 01:28:50.520
but because it's out of context.

01:28:50.520 --> 01:28:53.040
So what do you think's
happening to language?

01:28:53.040 --> 01:28:56.100
Are we getting better at
speaking, worse at speaking?

01:28:56.100 --> 01:28:58.260
And what do you think the role of things

01:28:58.260 --> 01:28:59.700
like texting and tweeting

01:28:59.700 --> 01:29:02.970
and shorthand communication, hashtagging,

01:29:02.970 --> 01:29:05.310
what's that doing to the
way that our brains work?

01:29:05.310 --> 01:29:07.143
- Yeah, I think that,

01:29:08.010 --> 01:29:10.533
well, one, in terms of, you know,

01:29:11.550 --> 01:29:14.370
measuring your level of
sophistication and intelligence

01:29:14.370 --> 01:29:16.590
when you say OMG, right.

01:29:16.590 --> 01:29:19.080
I think that also could
be a cultural thing

01:29:19.080 --> 01:29:21.930
that, ah, you belong
to the next generation.

01:29:21.930 --> 01:29:22.980
If you're an, you know,

01:29:22.980 --> 01:29:23.910
or you're being cool,

01:29:23.910 --> 01:29:25.980
if you're an older person, you know,

01:29:25.980 --> 01:29:29.040
using OMG and other
things that the, you know,

01:29:29.040 --> 01:29:31.050
younger generation would use.

01:29:31.050 --> 01:29:34.383
But if I really think about it clearly,

01:29:36.330 --> 01:29:39.330
texting actually has allowed

01:29:39.330 --> 01:29:43.083
for more rapid communication
amongst people.

01:29:44.119 --> 01:29:47.670
I think, without the invention
of the phone before then,

01:29:47.670 --> 01:29:50.100
or, you know, texting back and forth,

01:29:50.100 --> 01:29:53.370
you had to wait days
for a letter to show up.

01:29:53.370 --> 01:29:54.810
You couldn't call somebody on the phone

01:29:54.810 --> 01:29:55.947
and talk as well, you know?

01:29:55.947 --> 01:29:57.870
And so this rapid communication

01:29:57.870 --> 01:30:01.590
in terms of the rapid communication
of writing in this case.

01:30:01.590 --> 01:30:04.020
So I think actually,

01:30:04.020 --> 01:30:06.540
it's more like a use it or lose it

01:30:06.540 --> 01:30:10.140
kind of a thing with the brain.

01:30:10.140 --> 01:30:13.620
The more you use a particular
brain region or circuit,

01:30:13.620 --> 01:30:14.520
the more enhanced.

01:30:14.520 --> 01:30:16.320
It's like a muscle.

01:30:16.320 --> 01:30:19.110
The more you exercise it,
the more healthier it is,

01:30:19.110 --> 01:30:21.210
the bigger it becomes and
the more space it takes

01:30:21.210 --> 01:30:23.370
and the more you lose something else.

01:30:23.370 --> 01:30:28.370
So I think texting is not decreasing

01:30:30.540 --> 01:30:32.040
the speech prowess,

01:30:32.040 --> 01:30:34.230
or the intellectual prowess of speech.

01:30:34.230 --> 01:30:38.043
It's converting it and using
it a lot in a different way,

01:30:38.940 --> 01:30:43.260
in a way that may not be
as rich in regular writing,

01:30:43.260 --> 01:30:47.550
because you can only
communicate so much nuance

01:30:47.550 --> 01:30:49.620
in short-term writing,

01:30:49.620 --> 01:30:53.190
but whatever is being done,

01:30:53.190 --> 01:30:55.290
you got people texting hours and hours

01:30:55.290 --> 01:30:56.550
and hours on the phone.

01:30:56.550 --> 01:31:00.360
So whatever, your thumb circuit
is going to get pretty big,

01:31:00.360 --> 01:31:02.010
actually. [laughs]

01:31:02.010 --> 01:31:04.290
- I do wonder whether, you know,

01:31:04.290 --> 01:31:07.080
many people have lost
their jobs based on tweets.

01:31:07.080 --> 01:31:08.100
- [Erich] Hmm hmm.

01:31:08.100 --> 01:31:11.550
- The short latency
between thought and action

01:31:11.550 --> 01:31:13.830
and distribution of one's thoughts

01:31:13.830 --> 01:31:15.930
is incredible.
- Yes.

01:31:15.930 --> 01:31:18.700
- And I'm not just talking about people

01:31:20.070 --> 01:31:23.970
who apparently would have poor
prefrontal top-down control.

01:31:23.970 --> 01:31:25.260
This is geek speak by the way,

01:31:25.260 --> 01:31:27.360
for people that lack impulse control.

01:31:27.360 --> 01:31:28.860
But high-level academics,

01:31:28.860 --> 01:31:30.540
I'm not going to point fingers at anyone.

01:31:30.540 --> 01:31:33.570
But examples of where you
see these tweets and you go,

01:31:33.570 --> 01:31:35.850
what were they thinking?
- Yep.

01:31:35.850 --> 01:31:39.810
- So presumably, there's
an optimal strategy

01:31:39.810 --> 01:31:44.400
between the thought speech motor pathway,

01:31:44.400 --> 01:31:47.340
especially when the motor
pathway engages communication

01:31:47.340 --> 01:31:49.170
with hundreds of thousands of people

01:31:49.170 --> 01:31:50.640
and retweets in particular

01:31:50.640 --> 01:31:51.473
and the cut and paste function

01:31:51.473 --> 01:31:53.220
and the screenshot function

01:31:53.220 --> 01:31:55.680
are often the reason
why speech propagates.

01:31:55.680 --> 01:31:56.513
- [Erich] Yep.

01:31:56.513 --> 01:31:59.223
- So to me, it's a little eerie that,

01:32:00.600 --> 01:32:04.320
just that the neural circuitry can do this

01:32:04.320 --> 01:32:08.850
and that we are catching
up a little bit more slowly

01:32:08.850 --> 01:32:10.140
to the technology,

01:32:10.140 --> 01:32:13.800
and you've got these
casualties of that mismatch.

01:32:13.800 --> 01:32:18.800
- I think that's a good
adjective used, the casualties,

01:32:19.320 --> 01:32:20.370
you know, of what's going on,

01:32:20.370 --> 01:32:23.310
because yes, it is the case with texting,

01:32:23.310 --> 01:32:25.470
what you're really losing there

01:32:25.470 --> 01:32:28.320
is less so the ability to write,

01:32:28.320 --> 01:32:32.010
but more the ability to
interpret what is being written.

01:32:32.010 --> 01:32:33.750
And you can over or under interpret

01:32:33.750 --> 01:32:36.393
something that somebody means.

01:32:37.650 --> 01:32:40.200
On the flip side of that, you know,

01:32:40.200 --> 01:32:42.453
if somebody's writing
something very quick,

01:32:43.590 --> 01:32:46.110
they could be writing instinctually,

01:32:46.110 --> 01:32:49.290
more instinctually, their true meaning,

01:32:49.290 --> 01:32:51.840
and they don't have time to modify

01:32:51.840 --> 01:32:55.050
and color code what they're trying to say.

01:32:55.050 --> 01:32:57.720
And that's what they really feel

01:32:57.720 --> 01:33:00.210
and as opposed to saying
it in a more nuanced way.

01:33:00.210 --> 01:33:05.040
So I think both sides of
that casualty are present.

01:33:05.040 --> 01:33:07.350
And that's a downturn, you know,

01:33:07.350 --> 01:33:12.350
unintended negative
consequence of short-term,

01:33:12.510 --> 01:33:14.820
I mean, short-word communications.

01:33:14.820 --> 01:33:17.400
- Yeah, I agree that this whole phenomenon

01:33:17.400 --> 01:33:20.580
could be netting people that normally

01:33:20.580 --> 01:33:22.290
would only say these things out loud

01:33:22.290 --> 01:33:24.150
once inside the door of their own home.

01:33:24.150 --> 01:33:25.110
- Right.
- Or not at all.

01:33:25.110 --> 01:33:26.370
- [Erich] Right.

01:33:26.370 --> 01:33:28.290
- It's an interesting time that we're in.

01:33:28.290 --> 01:33:31.172
These are these speech and
language and motor patterns.

01:33:31.172 --> 01:33:32.880
- So part of the human
evolution for language,

01:33:32.880 --> 01:33:35.310
I think this is all part of our evolution.

01:33:35.310 --> 01:33:36.722
- That's right.
- Yeah.

01:33:36.722 --> 01:33:38.250
- So for those of you
thinking terrible thoughts,

01:33:38.250 --> 01:33:40.200
please put them in the
world and be a casualty.

01:33:40.200 --> 01:33:41.520
And for those of you that are not,

01:33:41.520 --> 01:33:44.160
please be very careful with how proficient

01:33:44.160 --> 01:33:47.085
your thought to language
to motor action goes.

01:33:47.085 --> 01:33:47.970
- [Erich] Yes. [laughs]

01:33:47.970 --> 01:33:49.380
- Maybe the technology companies

01:33:49.380 --> 01:33:52.650
should install some buffers,
some AI-based buffers.

01:33:52.650 --> 01:33:54.810
- Right, that's taking some EEG signals

01:33:54.810 --> 01:33:56.850
from your brain while
you're texting to say,

01:33:56.850 --> 01:34:00.990
okay, this is not a
great thought, slow down.

01:34:00.990 --> 01:34:03.993
- Right, or this doesn't
reflect your best state.

01:34:04.860 --> 01:34:06.180
That brings me to

01:34:06.180 --> 01:34:08.220
what was going to be the
next question anyway,

01:34:08.220 --> 01:34:10.890
which is we are quickly
moving toward a time

01:34:10.890 --> 01:34:15.240
where there will be an
even faster transition

01:34:15.240 --> 01:34:18.600
from thought to speech, to motor output,

01:34:18.600 --> 01:34:20.760
and maybe won't require motor output.

01:34:20.760 --> 01:34:21.630
What I'm referring to here

01:34:21.630 --> 01:34:24.300
is some of the incredible
work of our colleagues,

01:34:24.300 --> 01:34:26.010
Eddie Chang at UCSF and others

01:34:26.010 --> 01:34:29.550
who are taking paralyzed human beings

01:34:29.550 --> 01:34:32.490
and learning to translate
the electrical signals

01:34:32.490 --> 01:34:33.810
of neurons in various areas,

01:34:33.810 --> 01:34:35.520
including speech and language areas,

01:34:35.520 --> 01:34:37.500
to computer screens that type out

01:34:37.500 --> 01:34:38.400
what these people are thinking.

01:34:38.400 --> 01:34:39.233
In other words,

01:34:39.233 --> 01:34:42.330
paralyzed people can put
their thoughts into writing.

01:34:42.330 --> 01:34:45.210
That's a pretty extreme
and wonderful example

01:34:45.210 --> 01:34:47.700
of recovery of function.
- Hmm hmm.

01:34:47.700 --> 01:34:50.340
- That is sure to continue to evolve.

01:34:50.340 --> 01:34:52.830
But I think we are headed toward a time,

01:34:52.830 --> 01:34:54.000
not too long from now

01:34:54.000 --> 01:34:58.530
where my thoughts can be
translated into words on a page

01:34:58.530 --> 01:35:00.450
if I allow that to happen.

01:35:00.450 --> 01:35:03.000
- Yeah so, and Eddie Chang's work,

01:35:03.000 --> 01:35:06.363
which I admire quite a
bit and cite in my papers,

01:35:07.200 --> 01:35:10.170
I think he's really one of
those at the leading edge

01:35:10.170 --> 01:35:13.470
of trying to understand within humans,

01:35:13.470 --> 01:35:15.600
the neurobiology of speech.

01:35:15.600 --> 01:35:17.820
And he may not say it
directly, but you know,

01:35:17.820 --> 01:35:18.990
I talked to him about this.

01:35:18.990 --> 01:35:21.960
It supports this idea
that the speech circuit

01:35:21.960 --> 01:35:23.610
and the separate language module,

01:35:23.610 --> 01:35:26.850
I don't really think that
there's a separation there.

01:35:26.850 --> 01:35:28.800
So with that knowledge,

01:35:28.800 --> 01:35:31.320
yes, and putting
electrodes into human brain

01:35:31.320 --> 01:35:33.390
and then translating
those electrical signals

01:35:33.390 --> 01:35:34.830
to speech currents.

01:35:34.830 --> 01:35:38.250
Yeah, we can start to tell
what is that person thinking?

01:35:38.250 --> 01:35:41.223
Why, because we often
think in terms of speech.

01:35:42.210 --> 01:35:44.970
And without saying words.

01:35:44.970 --> 01:35:46.500
And that's a scary thought.

01:35:46.500 --> 01:35:49.320
And now imagine if you can now translate

01:35:49.320 --> 01:35:52.620
those into a signal that
transmits something wirelessly

01:35:52.620 --> 01:35:55.650
and someone from some
distant part of the planet

01:35:55.650 --> 01:35:58.710
is hearing your speech
from a wireless signal

01:35:58.710 --> 01:35:59.823
without you speaking.

01:36:00.960 --> 01:36:04.770
So probably that won't be
done in an ethical way,

01:36:04.770 --> 01:36:05.770
who knows, you know?

01:36:06.854 --> 01:36:08.970
But I mean, the ethics
of doing that probably,

01:36:08.970 --> 01:36:11.100
you know, might not happen, but who knows?

01:36:11.100 --> 01:36:13.774
We have these songbirds, you know.

01:36:13.774 --> 01:36:15.000
If we apply the same technique to them,

01:36:15.000 --> 01:36:16.410
we can start to hear what they're singing

01:36:16.410 --> 01:36:18.480
in their dreams or whatever,

01:36:18.480 --> 01:36:19.800
even though they don't produce sound

01:36:19.800 --> 01:36:22.350
so we can find out by testing on them.

01:36:22.350 --> 01:36:23.280
- It's coming.
- Yes.

01:36:23.280 --> 01:36:24.980
- One way or another, it's coming.

01:36:26.130 --> 01:36:28.230
For those listening who are interested

01:36:28.230 --> 01:36:32.490
in getting better at speaking
and understanding languages,

01:36:32.490 --> 01:36:34.740
are there any tools that you recommend?

01:36:34.740 --> 01:36:36.354
And here again,

01:36:36.354 --> 01:36:37.860
I realize you're not a speech therapist,

01:36:37.860 --> 01:36:39.120
but here I'm not thinking about

01:36:39.120 --> 01:36:42.240
ameliorating any kind
of speech deficiency.

01:36:42.240 --> 01:36:44.070
I'm thinking, for instance,

01:36:44.070 --> 01:36:46.770
do you recommend that people read

01:36:46.770 --> 01:36:48.603
different types of writing?

01:36:49.470 --> 01:36:51.420
Would you recommend that
people learn how to dance

01:36:51.420 --> 01:36:54.420
in order to become better at
expressing themselves verbally?

01:36:55.500 --> 01:37:00.480
You know, and feel free to
have some degrees of freedom

01:37:00.480 --> 01:37:01.590
in this answer.

01:37:01.590 --> 01:37:04.200
These are obviously not
peer-reviewed studies

01:37:04.200 --> 01:37:07.140
that we're referring to,
although there may be,

01:37:07.140 --> 01:37:10.110
but I'm struck by the number of things

01:37:10.110 --> 01:37:11.520
that you do exceedingly well,

01:37:11.520 --> 01:37:13.649
and I can't help but ask,

01:37:13.649 --> 01:37:17.700
well, the singing, which I realize it may,

01:37:17.700 --> 01:37:18.960
your brother didn't pay me to say this,

01:37:18.960 --> 01:37:21.420
may not be quite as good
as your brothers yet,

01:37:21.420 --> 01:37:23.190
but is getting, you'll surpass him,

01:37:23.190 --> 01:37:24.353
I'm guessing at some point.

01:37:24.353 --> 01:37:25.254
♪ Getting there ♪

01:37:25.254 --> 01:37:26.087
- Getting there.

01:37:26.087 --> 01:37:26.920
[both laughing]

01:37:26.920 --> 01:37:28.203
Exactly, there you go.

01:37:29.670 --> 01:37:32.280
You know, should kids learn how to dance

01:37:32.280 --> 01:37:35.940
and read hard books and simple books?

01:37:35.940 --> 01:37:36.773
What do you recommend?

01:37:36.773 --> 01:37:38.070
Should adults learn how to do that?

01:37:38.070 --> 01:37:38.903
Everyone wants to know how

01:37:38.903 --> 01:37:41.760
to keep their brain working
better, so to speak.

01:37:41.760 --> 01:37:44.610
But also I think people want
to be able to speak well

01:37:44.610 --> 01:37:46.620
and people want to be
able to understand well.

01:37:46.620 --> 01:37:50.580
- Yeah, so what I've
discovered personally, right,

01:37:50.580 --> 01:37:52.350
is that, so when I switched

01:37:52.350 --> 01:37:57.350
from pursuing a career in
science from a career in dance,

01:37:59.610 --> 01:38:01.743
I thought one day I would stop dancing,

01:38:02.580 --> 01:38:06.870
but I haven't because I
find it fulfilling for me,

01:38:06.870 --> 01:38:09.180
you know, just as a life experience.

01:38:09.180 --> 01:38:11.760
So ever since I started college,

01:38:11.760 --> 01:38:15.360
you know, my late teens
and early twenties,

01:38:15.360 --> 01:38:18.390
I kept dancing even till this day.

01:38:18.390 --> 01:38:19.500
And there've been periods of time,

01:38:19.500 --> 01:38:21.000
like during the pandemic

01:38:21.000 --> 01:38:23.253
where I slowed down on
dancing and so forth.

01:38:24.300 --> 01:38:26.430
And when you do that, you realize, okay,

01:38:26.430 --> 01:38:27.780
there are parts of your body

01:38:27.780 --> 01:38:30.694
where your muscle tone decreases
a little bit and somewhat,

01:38:30.694 --> 01:38:32.640
or you could start to gain weight.

01:38:32.640 --> 01:38:34.290
I somehow don't gain weight that easily.

01:38:34.290 --> 01:38:35.880
And I think it's related to my dance,

01:38:35.880 --> 01:38:38.970
if that's meaningful to your audience.

01:38:38.970 --> 01:38:41.643
But what I found is, you know,

01:38:42.557 --> 01:38:44.790
in science, we like to
think of a separation

01:38:44.790 --> 01:38:48.300
between movement and action and cognition.

01:38:48.300 --> 01:38:49.650
And there is a separation for you

01:38:49.650 --> 01:38:51.660
between perception and production,

01:38:51.660 --> 01:38:53.580
cognition being perception,

01:38:53.580 --> 01:38:55.830
production being moving, right.

01:38:55.830 --> 01:38:57.840
But if the speech pathways

01:38:57.840 --> 01:38:59.820
is next to the movement pathways,

01:38:59.820 --> 01:39:02.340
what I discover is by dancing,

01:39:02.340 --> 01:39:04.950
it is helping me think.

01:39:04.950 --> 01:39:07.200
It is helping keeping my brain fresh.

01:39:07.200 --> 01:39:09.270
It's not just moving my muscles,

01:39:09.270 --> 01:39:13.050
I'm moving or using the
circuitry in my brain

01:39:13.050 --> 01:39:16.440
to control a whole big body.

01:39:16.440 --> 01:39:18.750
You need a lot of brain tissue to do that.

01:39:18.750 --> 01:39:19.890
And so I argue,

01:39:19.890 --> 01:39:24.890
if you want to stay cognitively
intact into your old age,

01:39:25.230 --> 01:39:26.550
you better be moving

01:39:26.550 --> 01:39:28.380
and you better be doing it consistently,

01:39:28.380 --> 01:39:31.140
whether it's dancing, walking, running,

01:39:31.140 --> 01:39:34.410
and also practicing speech,

01:39:34.410 --> 01:39:36.840
oratory speech and so forth or singing,

01:39:36.840 --> 01:39:38.580
is controlling the brain circuits

01:39:38.580 --> 01:39:40.470
that are moving your facial musculature.

01:39:40.470 --> 01:39:44.640
And it's going to keep your
cognitive circuits also in tune.

01:39:44.640 --> 01:39:47.970
And I'm convinced of that from
my own personal experience.

01:39:47.970 --> 01:39:50.880
- Yeah, for me, long, slow runs

01:39:50.880 --> 01:39:54.240
are a wonderful way to
kind of loosen the joints

01:39:54.240 --> 01:39:58.110
for long podcasts,
especially the solo podcast,

01:39:58.110 --> 01:40:00.120
which can take many hours to record.

01:40:00.120 --> 01:40:03.270
And without those long slow runs,

01:40:03.270 --> 01:40:05.820
at least the day before,
or even the morning of,

01:40:05.820 --> 01:40:08.190
I don't think I could do
it, at least not as well.

01:40:08.190 --> 01:40:10.740
- All right, well, you're
experiencing something similar.

01:40:10.740 --> 01:40:11.940
So that's an N of two.

01:40:11.940 --> 01:40:12.933
- Yeah, N of two.

01:40:13.770 --> 01:40:15.720
I'm tempted to learn how to dance

01:40:15.720 --> 01:40:18.218
because there are a lot of
reasons to learn how to dance.

01:40:18.218 --> 01:40:19.051
- [Erich] Yes.

01:40:19.051 --> 01:40:21.060
- And people can use their imagination.

01:40:21.060 --> 01:40:23.640
I definitely want to get the opportunity

01:40:23.640 --> 01:40:25.890
to talk about some of the newer work

01:40:25.890 --> 01:40:30.870
that you're into right now
about genomes of animals.

01:40:30.870 --> 01:40:32.430
As you perhaps can tell

01:40:32.430 --> 01:40:35.070
from my quite authentic
facial expressions,

01:40:35.070 --> 01:40:37.920
I adore the animal kingdom.

01:40:37.920 --> 01:40:39.120
I just find it amazing.

01:40:39.120 --> 01:40:41.853
And it's the reason I went
into neurobiology, in part.

01:40:42.930 --> 01:40:45.420
So many animals, so many
different patterns of movement,

01:40:45.420 --> 01:40:49.410
so many body plans, so
many specializations,

01:40:49.410 --> 01:40:51.960
what is the value of learning the genomes

01:40:51.960 --> 01:40:53.850
of all these animals?

01:40:53.850 --> 01:40:58.230
You know, I can think of
conservation-based, you know,

01:40:58.230 --> 01:41:02.100
schemes of trying to preserve
these precious critters,

01:41:02.100 --> 01:41:04.170
but what are you doing with
the genomes of these animals?

01:41:04.170 --> 01:41:06.537
What do you want to understand
about their brain circuits?

01:41:06.537 --> 01:41:08.430
And how does this relate
to some of the discussion

01:41:08.430 --> 01:41:09.330
we've have been having up until now?

01:41:09.330 --> 01:41:13.293
- Yeah, I've gotten very
heavily involved in genomes,

01:41:14.400 --> 01:41:16.980
you know, not just to
get at an individual gene

01:41:16.980 --> 01:41:21.480
involved in the trade of
interest, like spoken language,

01:41:21.480 --> 01:41:24.780
but I realize that, you know,

01:41:24.780 --> 01:41:27.400
nature has done natural experiments for us

01:41:28.620 --> 01:41:31.620
with all these species out
there with these various traits

01:41:31.620 --> 01:41:33.840
and the one that I'm
studying, like vocal learning,

01:41:33.840 --> 01:41:36.480
has evolved multiple times
among the animal kingdom,

01:41:36.480 --> 01:41:39.510
even if it's rare, it's multiple times.

01:41:39.510 --> 01:41:44.510
And the similar genetic changes
occurred in those species.

01:41:45.810 --> 01:41:48.390
But to find out what those genetic changes

01:41:48.390 --> 01:41:50.580
that are associated with
the trait of interests

01:41:50.580 --> 01:41:53.400
and not some other trait
like flying in birds,

01:41:53.400 --> 01:41:55.503
as opposed to singing,

01:41:56.490 --> 01:41:58.830
you have to do what's
called comparative genomics,

01:41:58.830 --> 01:42:01.017
even in the context of studying the brain.

01:42:01.017 --> 01:42:04.170
And you need their genomes
to compare the genomes

01:42:04.170 --> 01:42:07.200
and do like a GWA, a
genome-wide association study,

01:42:07.200 --> 01:42:10.620
not just within a species like
humans, but across species.

01:42:10.620 --> 01:42:13.530
And so you need good genomes to do that.

01:42:13.530 --> 01:42:16.230
Plus, I've discovered I'm also interested

01:42:16.230 --> 01:42:17.880
in evolution and origins.

01:42:17.880 --> 01:42:20.950
How did these species
come about a similar trait

01:42:22.350 --> 01:42:25.950
in last, you know, 300 million
years or 60 million years,

01:42:25.950 --> 01:42:27.720
depending who you're talking about.

01:42:27.720 --> 01:42:30.900
And you need a good
phylogenetic tree to do that,

01:42:30.900 --> 01:42:32.640
and to get a good phylogenetic tree,

01:42:32.640 --> 01:42:34.590
you also need their genomes.

01:42:34.590 --> 01:42:35.940
And so, because of this,

01:42:35.940 --> 01:42:38.640
I got involved in large scale consortiums

01:42:38.640 --> 01:42:41.820
to produce genomes of
many different species,

01:42:41.820 --> 01:42:43.230
including my vocal learners

01:42:43.230 --> 01:42:46.353
and their closest
relatives that I'm fans of.

01:42:47.400 --> 01:42:49.230
But I couldn't convince
the funding agencies

01:42:49.230 --> 01:42:52.620
to gimme the money to do
that just for my own project.

01:42:52.620 --> 01:42:55.350
But when you get a whole
bunch of people together

01:42:55.350 --> 01:42:57.210
who want to study various traits,

01:42:57.210 --> 01:42:59.430
you know, heart disease,

01:42:59.430 --> 01:43:03.510
or loss and gain in flight and so forth,

01:43:03.510 --> 01:43:06.840
suddenly we all need lots
of genomes to do this.

01:43:06.840 --> 01:43:09.690
And so now that got me into a project

01:43:09.690 --> 01:43:12.660
to lead something called the
Vertebrate Genomes Project

01:43:12.660 --> 01:43:16.740
to eventually sequence all
70,000 species on the planet.

01:43:16.740 --> 01:43:18.900
And Earth BioGenome Project,

01:43:18.900 --> 01:43:22.023
all eukaryotic species,
all two million of them.

01:43:23.160 --> 01:43:26.790
And to no longer be in a situation

01:43:26.790 --> 01:43:28.950
where I wish I had this genome.

01:43:28.950 --> 01:43:32.310
Now we have the genetic code
of all life on the planet,

01:43:32.310 --> 01:43:34.620
create a database of all their traits

01:43:34.620 --> 01:43:36.600
and find the genetic association

01:43:36.600 --> 01:43:38.970
with everything out there

01:43:38.970 --> 01:43:41.570
that makes a difference
from one species to another.

01:43:42.450 --> 01:43:47.400
One more piece of the
equation to add to this story

01:43:47.400 --> 01:43:50.880
is what I didn't realize
as a neuroscientist

01:43:50.880 --> 01:43:55.170
were that these genomes
are not only incomplete,

01:43:55.170 --> 01:43:58.230
but have lots of errors in them,

01:43:58.230 --> 01:44:00.150
false gene duplications,

01:44:00.150 --> 01:44:01.800
where mother and father chromosomes

01:44:01.800 --> 01:44:03.930
were so different from each other,

01:44:03.930 --> 01:44:05.580
that the genome algorithm,

01:44:05.580 --> 01:44:08.340
assembly algorithms treated
them as two different genes

01:44:08.340 --> 01:44:10.500
in this part of the chromosome.

01:44:10.500 --> 01:44:12.900
So there are a lot of these
false duplicated genes

01:44:12.900 --> 01:44:16.170
that people thought
were real, but were not

01:44:16.170 --> 01:44:18.480
or missing parts of the genome

01:44:18.480 --> 01:44:21.750
because the enzymes
used to sequence the DNA

01:44:21.750 --> 01:44:23.850
couldn't get through
this regulatory region

01:44:23.850 --> 01:44:26.160
that folded up on itself

01:44:26.160 --> 01:44:28.560
and made it hard to sequence.

01:44:28.560 --> 01:44:31.720
And so I ended up in these consortiums

01:44:32.700 --> 01:44:35.640
pulling in the genome
sequencing companies,

01:44:35.640 --> 01:44:38.730
developing the technology to work with us

01:44:38.730 --> 01:44:40.050
to improve it further

01:44:40.050 --> 01:44:42.270
and the computer science guys

01:44:42.270 --> 01:44:44.550
who then take that data
and that technology,

01:44:44.550 --> 01:44:46.290
and try to make the complete genomes

01:44:46.290 --> 01:44:48.300
and make the algorithms better

01:44:48.300 --> 01:44:51.870
to produce what we now just did recently

01:44:51.870 --> 01:44:53.790
led by an effort by Adam Phillippy

01:44:53.790 --> 01:44:56.520
is the first human
Telomere-to-Telomere Genome

01:44:56.520 --> 01:45:00.750
with no errors, all complete,
no missing sequence.

01:45:00.750 --> 01:45:04.020
And now we're trying to do the
same thing with vertebrates

01:45:04.020 --> 01:45:04.853
and other species.

01:45:04.853 --> 01:45:07.263
Actually, we improved
that before we got to the,

01:45:07.263 --> 01:45:09.030
what we call telomere-to-telomere,

01:45:09.030 --> 01:45:11.220
from one end of the chromosome to another.

01:45:11.220 --> 01:45:13.590
And what we're discovering

01:45:13.590 --> 01:45:15.780
is in this dark matter of the genome

01:45:15.780 --> 01:45:17.670
that was missing before,

01:45:17.670 --> 01:45:20.550
turns out to be some regulatory regions

01:45:20.550 --> 01:45:23.340
that are specialized in
vocal learning species

01:45:23.340 --> 01:45:26.580
and we think are involved in
developing speech circuits.

01:45:26.580 --> 01:45:27.413
- Incredible.

01:45:27.413 --> 01:45:29.700
Well, so much to learn.

01:45:29.700 --> 01:45:32.130
And we're going to learn
from this information.

01:45:32.130 --> 01:45:35.280
Early on in these genome
projects and connectome projects,

01:45:35.280 --> 01:45:37.020
I confess I was a little bit cynical.

01:45:37.020 --> 01:45:39.060
This would be about 10, 15 years ago.

01:45:39.060 --> 01:45:40.380
I thought, okay, necessary,

01:45:40.380 --> 01:45:42.630
but not sufficient for anything.

01:45:42.630 --> 01:45:44.580
We need it, but it's not
clear what's going to happen,

01:45:44.580 --> 01:45:46.230
but you just gave a very clear example

01:45:46.230 --> 01:45:49.620
of what we stand to learn
from this kind of information.

01:45:49.620 --> 01:45:52.590
And I know from the conservation side,

01:45:52.590 --> 01:45:53.790
there's a huge interest in this

01:45:53.790 --> 01:45:55.410
because even though we would prefer

01:45:55.410 --> 01:45:58.800
to keep all these species
alive rather than clone them,

01:45:58.800 --> 01:46:01.320
these sorts of projects
do offer the possibility

01:46:01.320 --> 01:46:04.230
of potentially recreating
species that were lost.

01:46:04.230 --> 01:46:05.063
- [Erich] Right.

01:46:05.063 --> 01:46:09.360
- Due to our own ignorance or
missteps, or what have you.

01:46:09.360 --> 01:46:12.153
- Yes, and along those lines,

01:46:13.440 --> 01:46:16.110
because, you know, we
got involved in genomics,

01:46:16.110 --> 01:46:19.350
some of the first species
that we start working on

01:46:19.350 --> 01:46:21.540
are critically endangered species.

01:46:21.540 --> 01:46:25.860
And I'm doing that not only for, you know,

01:46:25.860 --> 01:46:27.510
perspectives to understand their brains

01:46:27.510 --> 01:46:29.370
and the genes involved
in their brain function,

01:46:29.370 --> 01:46:31.800
but I feel like it's a moral duty.

01:46:31.800 --> 01:46:34.560
So the fact that now
I become more involved

01:46:34.560 --> 01:46:36.090
in genome biology

01:46:36.090 --> 01:46:37.650
and have helped develop these tools

01:46:37.650 --> 01:46:39.030
for more complete genomes,

01:46:39.030 --> 01:46:43.830
let's capture their genetic
code now, before they're gone.

01:46:43.830 --> 01:46:46.560
And could we use that information

01:46:46.560 --> 01:46:49.440
to resurrect the species
at some future time,

01:46:49.440 --> 01:46:50.940
if not in my lifetime,

01:46:50.940 --> 01:46:54.780
in some time in the future
and generations ahead of us.

01:46:54.780 --> 01:46:59.550
And so, in anticipation of that,

01:46:59.550 --> 01:47:01.990
we create a database,
we call the GenomeArk

01:47:03.060 --> 01:47:05.940
and no pun intended like Noah's Ark,

01:47:05.940 --> 01:47:08.310
meant to store the genetic code

01:47:08.310 --> 01:47:10.830
as complete genome assemblies as possible

01:47:10.830 --> 01:47:13.200
for all species on the planet

01:47:13.200 --> 01:47:14.670
to be used for basic science,

01:47:14.670 --> 01:47:17.310
but also some point in the future.

01:47:17.310 --> 01:47:19.380
And because of that,

01:47:19.380 --> 01:47:21.690
funding agencies or private foundations

01:47:21.690 --> 01:47:23.460
that are interested in conservation

01:47:23.460 --> 01:47:26.640
have been reaching out to
me now, a neuroscientist,

01:47:26.640 --> 01:47:30.360
to help them out in producing
high quality genome data

01:47:30.360 --> 01:47:32.430
of endangered species that they can use,

01:47:32.430 --> 01:47:34.140
like Revive &amp; Restore,

01:47:34.140 --> 01:47:36.540
who want to resurrect the passenger pigeon

01:47:36.540 --> 01:47:39.750
or Colossal, who wants to
resurrect the wooly mammoth.

01:47:39.750 --> 01:47:41.970
And so we're producing
high quality genomes

01:47:41.970 --> 01:47:44.700
for these groups, for the
conservation projects.

01:47:44.700 --> 01:47:47.250
- What a terrific and
important initiative.

01:47:47.250 --> 01:47:49.110
And I think for those listening today,

01:47:49.110 --> 01:47:52.150
they now certainly understand the value

01:47:53.311 --> 01:47:56.490
of deeply understanding
the brain structures

01:47:56.490 --> 01:47:58.410
and genomes of different species.

01:47:58.410 --> 01:48:00.330
Because I confess,

01:48:00.330 --> 01:48:02.820
even though I knew a bit
of the songbird literature,

01:48:02.820 --> 01:48:03.870
and I certainly understand

01:48:03.870 --> 01:48:05.460
that humans have speech and language,

01:48:05.460 --> 01:48:08.070
I had no idea that there
was so much convergence

01:48:08.070 --> 01:48:10.257
of function, structure and genomes.

01:48:10.257 --> 01:48:13.380
And to me, you know,
I feel a lot more like

01:48:13.380 --> 01:48:16.080
an ape than I do a songbird.
- Right.

01:48:16.080 --> 01:48:18.573
- And yet here we are
with the understanding

01:48:18.573 --> 01:48:20.400
that there's a lot more similarity

01:48:20.400 --> 01:48:22.680
between songbirds and humans

01:48:22.680 --> 01:48:24.390
than I certainly ever thought before.

01:48:24.390 --> 01:48:27.390
- Yeah, something very
close to home for us humans,

01:48:27.390 --> 01:48:31.173
I can give you an example of
is evolution of skin color.

01:48:32.010 --> 01:48:35.130
And skin color, we use it unfortunately,

01:48:35.130 --> 01:48:37.170
for racism and so forth.

01:48:37.170 --> 01:48:39.840
We use it also for good
things to let in more light

01:48:39.840 --> 01:48:42.900
or let out less light depending
on the part of the planet,

01:48:42.900 --> 01:48:45.360
you know, our population evolved in.

01:48:45.360 --> 01:48:47.610
And most people think dark-skinned people

01:48:47.610 --> 01:48:49.980
all evolved from the
same dark-skinned person

01:48:49.980 --> 01:48:51.660
and light-skinned people all evolved

01:48:51.660 --> 01:48:52.770
from the same light-skinned person,

01:48:52.770 --> 01:48:54.420
but that's not the case.

01:48:54.420 --> 01:48:57.450
Dark skin and light skin amongst humans

01:48:57.450 --> 01:49:00.630
has evolved independently multiple times,

01:49:00.630 --> 01:49:03.630
like in, you know, the
Pacific islands versus Africa.

01:49:03.630 --> 01:49:06.360
And it's just depending on the angle

01:49:06.360 --> 01:49:08.310
of light hitting the Earth

01:49:08.310 --> 01:49:10.650
as to whether you need more
protection from the sun

01:49:10.650 --> 01:49:12.690
or less protection,

01:49:12.690 --> 01:49:16.710
that's also associated with
Vitamin D synthesis in the skin.

01:49:16.710 --> 01:49:20.343
And so, and each time,

01:49:22.170 --> 01:49:26.100
where darker or lighter
skin evolved independently,

01:49:26.100 --> 01:49:28.710
it hit the same gene, you know,

01:49:28.710 --> 01:49:30.510
the mela [finger snaps].

01:49:30.510 --> 01:49:32.340
- Melanin.
- Melanin receptors.

01:49:32.340 --> 01:49:33.750
That's right, yes, yeah.

01:49:33.750 --> 01:49:37.080
Genes that are involved
in melanin in formation.

01:49:37.080 --> 01:49:41.010
And so those genes evolve
some of the same mutations,

01:49:41.010 --> 01:49:42.600
even in different species.

01:49:42.600 --> 01:49:44.250
It's not just humans.

01:49:44.250 --> 01:49:48.150
In equatorial regions, they
are darker-skinned animals

01:49:48.150 --> 01:49:50.520
than going away from the equator.

01:49:50.520 --> 01:49:52.140
- Oh, right, I think of Arctic foxes

01:49:52.140 --> 01:49:53.326
and things of that sort.
- Yep, that's right.

01:49:53.326 --> 01:49:54.953
That's right, polar bears, you know,

01:49:56.190 --> 01:50:00.180
and so some of the same genes are used

01:50:00.180 --> 01:50:03.090
in evolutionary perspective
to evolve in a similar way

01:50:03.090 --> 01:50:05.070
within and across species.

01:50:05.070 --> 01:50:06.120
- Incredible.
- Yeah.

01:50:06.120 --> 01:50:08.220
And that's same thing
happening in the brain too.

01:50:08.220 --> 01:50:09.900
